263
00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:16.550
And I quite frankly,

264
00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:18.630
10 years ago didn't know what Airbnb was.

265
00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:19.810
I was this hotelier

266
00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:22.230
who was about to get disrupted by Airbnb.

267
00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:24.450
And I didn't really know what this was.

268
00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:29.150
And he asked if I could be his in-house mentor

269
00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:31.330
and help this little tech startup

270
00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:34.380
become a global hospitality brand.

271
00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:39.380
And so I joined and quickly became known as the modern elder

272
00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:41.530
at Airbnb (chuckles).

273
00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:44.320
Which was not exactly the title I was looking for.

274
00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:45.153
Yeah.

275
00:11:45.153 --> 00:11:45.986
At age what?

276
00:11:45.986 --> 00:11:48.470
You were 52.
I was 52.

277
00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:51.830
Brian was 31, average age in the company was 26.

278
00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:52.680
So do the math.

279
00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:55.460
And they called me the modern elder

280
00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:57.020
partly because they said,

281
00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:58.697
Joe Gabby, one of the other co-founders said,

282
00:11:58.697 --> 00:12:01.230
"Chip, you're as curious as you are wise."

283
00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:02.063
And I was like,

284
00:12:02.063 --> 00:12:04.620
oh, well, I don't love the title modern elder.

285
00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:06.100
But if a modern elder is someone

286
00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.060
who's as curious as they are wise,

287
00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:11.037
and they know how to have the right alchemy of curiosity

288
00:12:11.037 --> 00:12:12.533
and wisdom in their life.

289
00:12:12.533 --> 00:12:14.520
That describes me pretty well.

290
00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:15.353
Sure.

291
00:12:15.353 --> 00:12:16.186
And I want to...

292
00:12:16.186 --> 00:12:17.019
Sorry to interrupt,

293
00:12:17.019 --> 00:12:18.640
but I do want to put a finer point

294
00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:23.630
on how you were able to kind of cultivate that curiosity

295
00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:27.160
in the wake of a successful exit from your company.

296
00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:28.260
Maybe we can come back to that.

297
00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:29.870
'Cause I think it is related to one other thing

298
00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:30.885
that you want to talk about.

299
00:12:30.885 --> 00:12:31.718
Yeah.

300
00:12:31.718 --> 00:12:32.890
I'll come back to that.

301
00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:35.200
And I'll just tell a little bit of that Airbnb story now.

302
00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.330
Is that sounds good.

303
00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:37.163
Yeah.

304
00:12:37.163 --> 00:12:39.660
So yes, I joined this place.

305
00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.660
I'd never been in a tech company before.

306
00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:45.090
I thought they wanted me for my knowledge

307
00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:46.590
of the hospitality industry.

308
00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:48.840
I mean, but frankly, it didn't really matter

309
00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:51.490
how many rooms made (indistinct) an eight hours shift

310
00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:53.810
in the Airbnb host world.

311
00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:58.680
So within, actually it was the third day on the job,

312
00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.447
I was in a meeting where Brian had said,

313
00:13:01.447 --> 00:13:04.767
"Listen, go spend a lot of time with the engineers.

314
00:13:04.767 --> 00:13:06.897
"You've never been in a tech company before.

315
00:13:06.897 --> 00:13:08.690
"Start learning the lingo."

316
00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:10.560
And so I thought I was supposed to be the mentor,

317
00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:13.590
but at the end of an hour meeting with a bunch of engineers,

318
00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:14.510
I knew I was the intern.

319
00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:17.180
I was like Robert de Niro,

320
00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:18.570
in the movie with Anne Hathaway.

321
00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:21.360
But he came in as the intern and he became the mentor.

322
00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:23.533
I actually came in as the mentor and now I'm the intern.

323
00:13:23.533 --> 00:13:27.000
And what I realized is that,

324
00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:28.280
I didn't understand the language,

325
00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.390
but that didn't mean I couldn't learn it.

326
00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:33.940
When I say the language, I meant like in that meeting,

327
00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:34.970
someone turned to me,

328
00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.350
the guy writing the meeting a 25-year-old guy turns to me

329
00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.857
and says, "Chip, if you ship a feature and no one used it,

330
00:13:40.857 --> 00:13:42.440
"did it really ship?"

331
00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:45.220
And I was like, I have no idea what you're talking about.

332
00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:48.260
Shipping a feature I've never worked in a tech company.

333
00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:53.260
So I had to acknowledge that I was not the smartest person

334
00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:56.220
in the room.

335
00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:57.740
I might have been the most curious person

336
00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:00.300
in the room at times and maybe the wisest,

337
00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.680
but I had to be open to learning.

338
00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:04.853
And my father said to me,

339
00:14:06.087 --> 00:14:11.087
"Chip, how could you turn your fear into curiosity?"

340
00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.400
And that was a really great reframe for me.

341
00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:18.750
Because I guess at 52 having run a company for 24 years,

342
00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:22.210
taking it from one person to 3,500 people.

343
00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:24.790
There was a part of me that in my early to mid 50s,

344
00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:29.330
I was thinking, oh, what if my last career step

345
00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:32.573
is a big mistake and I'm just a huge failure here?

346
00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:36.590
So I think part of what really helped,

347
00:14:36.590 --> 00:14:38.790
I was willing to be humble

348
00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:41.581
and vulnerable about what I didn't know.

349
00:14:41.581 --> 00:14:46.060
And often I was asking questions that helped the company

350
00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:49.525
to see their blind spots or see our blind spots.

351
00:14:49.525 --> 00:14:52.560
And I think it was that process

352
00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:55.000
that helped me to see that, gosh,

353
00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.240
more tech companies could use a modern elder.

354
00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:00.540
I just watched, we crashed.

355
00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:01.373
Right.

356
00:15:01.373 --> 00:15:03.050
And of course then there's "Super Pumped."

357
00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:08.050
And then there's the Inventor with Elizabeth Holmes.

358
00:15:08.094 --> 00:15:11.420
I mean, thank god, there's no Airbnb movie like that.

359
00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:12.390
This mini series,

360
00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:15.246
drama mini series of like these crash and burn tales.

361
00:15:15.246 --> 00:15:17.870
But if you look at it, none of those companies

362
00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:20.070
really had a modern elder.

363
00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:20.903
Well it's interesting

364
00:15:20.903 --> 00:15:22.680
'cause you wrote a blog post about this,

365
00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.724
on your Wisdom Well blog.

366
00:15:24.724 --> 00:15:25.557
Yeah.

367
00:15:25.557 --> 00:15:26.800
I watched all those founder series.

368
00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:30.490
Like I'm fascinated by them and they vary in quality.

369
00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:34.470
But the recurring theme is,

370
00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.520
ego gets the best of these people

371
00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:38.010
when it spirals outta control.

372
00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:42.730
But in the example of "Super Pumped,"

373
00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:45.170
which is about Travis Kanak and Uber,

374
00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:47.750
there actually was a modern elder in that context.

375
00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:49.580
They had Bill Gurley, who is the benchmark VC.

376
00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:52.180
Was sort of the stand-in for the modern elder,

377
00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:56.080
but he's butting up against an ego that is not having it.

378
00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:59.997
And that of course, sows the seeds for the downfall

379
00:15:59.997 --> 00:16:00.830
of his career.

380
00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:01.730
Yeah and the big difference for him...

381
00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:05.060
And you had Bruce Dunlevy for WeWork,

382
00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:07.830
who I went to business school with same class as me.

383
00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:12.830
But investors don't have time to be 24 hours a day

384
00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:14.980
in-house at the company.

385
00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:18.990
So Bill or Bruce will come to a meeting

386
00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:20.967
and say, "Okay, what's going on?"

387
00:16:20.967 --> 00:16:23.380
But then they go back and they look at the rest

388
00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:24.740
of their portfolio.

389
00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:27.270
And ultimately Bill got more involved in the business,

390
00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:31.940
but quite frankly, he could see what happens in a meeting.

391
00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:35.740
And of course you'd see some signs of serious hubris.

392
00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:38.695
And partly that's because Silicon Valley wants hubris.

393
00:16:38.695 --> 00:16:42.887
It's funny, there's an element of like Silicon Valley

394
00:16:42.887 --> 00:16:47.887
wants the confident, "Super Pumped" CEO founder.

395
00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:51.880
And then they want them to miraculously move

396
00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:53.220
from hubris to humility.

397
00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:54.490
Right-
As a leader.

398
00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:55.440
It's the package.

399
00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:58.350
Like if you want the guy who has the massive vision

400
00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:02.140
and the audacity to not only dream big, but execute on that,

401
00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:03.710
you're gonna get the other side of it.

402
00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:05.990
You are, and this is why...

403
00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:08.120
Brian Chesky, let's talk about him for a moment.

404
00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:09.927
When Brian approached me and said,

405
00:17:09.927 --> 00:17:14.177
"I want you to come in-house and full time at Airbnb,

406
00:17:14.177 --> 00:17:15.617
"and be my mentor,

407
00:17:15.617 --> 00:17:17.867
"but also be the head of global hospitality and strategy,

408
00:17:17.867 --> 00:17:19.870
"and a bunch of other things,"

409
00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:21.050
I had to look at him and say,

410
00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:23.040
okay, this guy, I'm 52, he's 31.

411
00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:26.160
I'm gonna report to him, but I'm gonna be his mentor.

412
00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:27.640
That's gonna be sort of odd.

413
00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.740
What's gonna be like to report to my mentee.

414
00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:33.940
And I had to have a level of confidence

415
00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:36.140
that he wanted to learn.

416
00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:39.840
And he had the hubris to go out and raise money,

417
00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:43.082
but he had the humility to build a culture

418
00:17:43.082 --> 00:17:45.048
and a leadership team.

419
00:17:45.048 --> 00:17:48.750
And to maybe even take some direction occasionally from me.

420
00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:49.583
Yeah.

421
00:17:49.583 --> 00:17:52.090
And so I think that was the key.

422
00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:53.740
And it's rare.
It is rare.

423
00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:55.420
That's why's-
And hats off to him-

424
00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:57.960
They're still the founders and it's functional.

425
00:17:57.960 --> 00:17:58.830
It's amazing.

426
00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:00.870
I mean we have three founders at Airbnb,

427
00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:03.190
it's over $100 billion company now.

428
00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:05.810
It's by far the most valuable hospitality company

429
00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:06.750
in the world.

430
00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:09.380
And the three founders are still very actively involved.

431
00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:10.620
That's never happened before.

432
00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:12.590
There's never been a company that has had that.

433
00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:13.880
And I'm proud of...

434
00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:16.300
I mean, I made all kinds of mistakes while I was there

435
00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:18.230
and the company would've done well without me.

436
00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:21.120
And yet I am very proud that the three of those founders

437
00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:22.800
are still working together as they are.

438
00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:26.590
Because I think the glue that needed to happen

439
00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:27.530
amongst the three,

440
00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:29.740
was partly between me and a couple other people

441
00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:32.880
who helped them to feel that sense

442
00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:35.330
of what each of them offered to the company

443
00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:36.320
and to each other.

444
00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:38.980
What's an example of some of the wisdom

445
00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:42.630
that you were able to impart and also the receptivity

446
00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:46.800
of Chesky and his team to what you had to offer.

447
00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:49.040
Sometimes it was as simple as...

448
00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:53.210
I mean, some of it was just little mechanical things.

449
00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:55.130
Because one of the things you get better at,

450
00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:58.470
as you get older is you've had the pattern recognition

451
00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:01.610
in your life to see what worked and what didn't.

452
00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:03.470
And so some of it was simple stuff

453
00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:05.900
like Brian who went into that meeting

454
00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:08.060
trying to sell the leadership team on something.

455
00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:10.830
And you knew there were two people in that meeting

456
00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:13.543
on the team who were gonna probably be critics of it.

457
00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:15.240
What would've been great

458
00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:17.070
is if you had spent some one-on-one time

459
00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:20.340
with each or both of them before the meeting,

460
00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.560
so they could actually air some of their concerns

461
00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:24.840
instead of feeling like

462
00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:27.540
they had to actually air it amongst everybody else.

463
00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:29.720
Because then you could have adapted the plan a little bit.

464
00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:30.650
So that's a simple thing.

465
00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:33.610
Or another simple thing would be like at Thanksgiving,

466
00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:37.510
we had all been running just crazy

467
00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:40.100
70, 80, 90-hour weeks flying everywhere,

468
00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:41.946
we're a global company.

469
00:19:41.946 --> 00:19:46.946
And the leadership team's families, weren't seeing us.

470
00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:51.130
And so it was Thanksgiving, and I said to Brian,

471
00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:54.570
what if you sent just a thank you note,

472
00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:58.250
maybe a bottle of wine or some flowers to the spouses

473
00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:01.040
and family members of all the leadership team?

474
00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:02.717
And just say, "This Thanksgiving,

475
00:20:02.717 --> 00:20:04.830
"I just wanna say thanks to you."

476
00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:07.690
Because what's happening behind the scenes, Brian is...

477
00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:10.980
Brian he wasn't married, he didn't have kids.

478
00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:11.960
But he didn't realize that.

479
00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:13.720
Yeah.
Others did.

480
00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:17.030
And behind the scenes, there's this person,

481
00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:21.070
the spouse saying, "I don't want you to work this much."

482
00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:24.020
And what we needed to really do is respect that.

483
00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:25.770
So those are a couple simple ideas.

484
00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:26.950
But WeWork...

485
00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:29.010
I mean, Adam Newman wanted to do

486
00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:32.710
a major strategic partnership with Airbnb.

487
00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:37.710
And Brian was sort of had some cognitive dissonance on it.

488
00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:40.700
He could see some of the value of it at one time.

489
00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:44.206
But I said, Brian this guy is a messiah.

490
00:20:44.206 --> 00:20:47.250
You've seen him act that way.

491
00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:50.330
How are you gonna create a logical business relationship

492
00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:52.593
with someone who believes they're the Messiah?

493
00:20:53.649 --> 00:20:56.153
I would say that a lot of my wisdom

494
00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:01.220
related to emotional intelligence.

495
00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:04.770
How do you develop emotional intelligence as a leader

496
00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:08.700
when you've had no background in being a leader?

497
00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:09.533
Yeah.

498
00:21:09.533 --> 00:21:14.100
Why do you think that Brian singled you out for this role?

499
00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:14.933
Like what...

500
00:21:14.933 --> 00:21:17.400
I mean, you have this robust history of being a mentor

501
00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:20.640
that started actually quite early in your life

502
00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.133
and in your career.

503
00:21:22.133 --> 00:21:26.900
But why do you think he had the foresight to see the need

504
00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:28.120
for a mentor

505
00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:31.690
and what was it about you that he found attractive?

506
00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:36.180
So, Brian is got the most voracious appetite

507
00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:38.420
for learning that I've ever met in my life.

508
00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:43.420
And so like when Airbnb in 2011 had this...

509
00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:46.690
Someone like totally trashed an apartment,

510
00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:50.320
he decided to go to the former head of the CIA.

511
00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:52.710
to learn about safety and security

512
00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:57.710
and how he could actually make the Airbnb platform safer.

513
00:21:58.060 --> 00:22:00.940
And so he had also had been talking

514
00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:02.860
to a guy named John Donahoe.

515
00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:04.170
So John's a friend of mine.

516
00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:06.430
And he and I were in YPO together.

517
00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:07.910
And we were in the same form.

518
00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:10.520
John's the CEO of Nike now.

519
00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:15.520
But back then, John was about 10 years ago, the eBay CEO.

520
00:22:15.550 --> 00:22:18.750
And so Brian, knowing that eBay had some similarities

521
00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:22.470
to Airbnb in terms of there was a two-sided marketplace,

522
00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:27.470
he was working with John as a mentor part-time.

523
00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:30.447
And so John, I think said to Brian,

524
00:22:30.447 --> 00:22:32.240
"You should talk to Chip."

525
00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:34.570
And Brian was like, well, tell me more about Chip.

526
00:22:34.570 --> 00:22:37.667
And so as Brian learned more, Brian said,

527
00:22:37.667 --> 00:22:39.597
"Oh man, I wanna learn from him."

528
00:22:39.597 --> 00:22:42.980
But I think what Brian initially was looking for

529
00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:46.440
was someone who had the knowledge

530
00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.500
of the hospitality and travel industry.

531
00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.223
Because when I joined the company,

532
00:22:50.223 --> 00:22:53.486
no one in the company had that background at all.

533
00:22:53.486 --> 00:22:57.135
But I think what he learned over time was he said,

534
00:22:57.135 --> 00:22:59.187
"I hired you for your knowledge,

535
00:22:59.187 --> 00:23:01.397
"but what I've really gotten was your wisdom.

536
00:23:01.397 --> 00:23:03.467
"And it's your wisdom of understanding people

537
00:23:03.467 --> 00:23:05.820
"and leadership and strategy."

538
00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:09.690
So I think one of the things we get better at

539
00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:11.860
as we get older is holistic thinking.

540
00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:13.577
Arthur Brooks talks about this in his new book,

541
00:23:13.577 --> 00:23:14.530
"From Strength to Strength."

542
00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:15.439
Yeah.

543
00:23:15.439 --> 00:23:16.272
He's coming in soon.

544
00:23:16.272 --> 00:23:17.105
Oh, is he?

545
00:23:17.105 --> 00:23:19.350
He's scheduled yeah.
Oh man, we are buddies.

546
00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:20.305
Yeah.

547
00:23:20.305 --> 00:23:22.700
You guys are like every time in researching like articles

548
00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:24.180
and stuff like that, it's like your name

549
00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:27.696
and his name always kind of are appearing in the same-

550
00:23:27.696 --> 00:23:28.529
I love...

551
00:23:28.529 --> 00:23:29.362
You know, he's so...

552
00:23:29.362 --> 00:23:30.300
He's like speaking of "Super Pumped."

553
00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:32.120
I mean, this is a guy who's adrenalized,

554
00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:36.366
and I really enjoy sometimes sparring with him,

555
00:23:36.366 --> 00:23:38.910
intellectual jousting.

556
00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:40.950
But in his book "From Strength to Strength,"

557
00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:43.740
he talks about going from fluid intelligence,

558
00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:45.440
to crystallized intelligence.

559
00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:47.040
Crystallized intelligence is...

560
00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:50.020
Fluid intelligence is very focused logical.

561
00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:53.230
And it's about actually innovating

562
00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:55.760
within a certain linear course.

563
00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:57.370
But fluid I'm sorry.

564
00:23:57.370 --> 00:24:01.100
Crystallized intelligence is when you are looking at things

565
00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:02.610
more holistically.

566
00:24:02.610 --> 00:24:04.760
Because one of the things that's interesting about our brain

567
00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:06.330
is as we age,

568
00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:10.470
we actually move from being very focused in the brain

569
00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:13.550
to actually having four wheel drive of the brain.

570
00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:16.750
We actually move much more lyrically and logically

571
00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:19.387
from one side, the right side to the left side.

572
00:24:19.387 --> 00:24:21.180
Which is great.

573
00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:23.690
Because what it means is that we can actually look

574
00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:25.070
systemically and holistically

575
00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:27.300
at things and connect the dots.

576
00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:29.640
It makes sense because you've accumulated

577
00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:32.490
so many experiences over the course of your life.

578
00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:35.450
You get to a point where you're able to synthesize

579
00:24:35.450 --> 00:24:36.620
all of that information.

580
00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:40.160
And I feel like just at 55 now,

581
00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:42.242
like an ability to kind of see things

582
00:24:42.242 --> 00:24:44.040
a little bit more clearly than I could-

583
00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:45.406
Yeah, I think...

584
00:24:45.406 --> 00:24:50.406
And whether it's wisdom or intuition, pattern recognition,

585
00:24:51.125 --> 00:24:53.360
you get to a place where

586
00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:56.057
the right answer just sort of pops up.

587
00:24:56.057 --> 00:24:59.250
And it's not because you've done some mathematical theory

588
00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:00.190
to get there.

589
00:25:00.190 --> 00:25:03.410
It's actually coming from your lived experience.

590
00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:07.344
I like to think of wisdom as metabolized experience

591
00:25:07.344 --> 00:25:10.600
which leads to distilled compassion.

592
00:25:10.600 --> 00:25:14.470
And so in both cases the metabolized of experience,

593
00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:16.850
how am I digesting my experience,

594
00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:20.050
leads to understanding yourself more, the world more,

595
00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:22.410
and then being able to have some level of compassion.

596
00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:24.364
'Cause I think wisdom without compassion

597
00:25:24.364 --> 00:25:28.766
is just doing something for yourself.

598
00:25:28.766 --> 00:25:32.950
One of the things I did early on, Rich,

599
00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:34.010
when I was 28 years old.

600
00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:36.630
So I started my boutique hotel company when I was 26.

601
00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:38.850
At 28, I realized I was clueless.

602
00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:40.644
I knew I was clueless at 26 too.

603
00:25:40.644 --> 00:25:44.520
'Cause I bought a motel in San Francisco.

604
00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:46.038
In a bad neighborhood.

605
00:25:46.038 --> 00:25:48.470
It was a pay by the hour motel.

606
00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:49.630
Which if you know what that means,

607
00:25:49.630 --> 00:25:51.810
it means it was very popular at lunchtime (chuckles).

608
00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:55.180
People would come and have their little affair at lunchtime

609
00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:58.080
and then go home or go back to the office.

610
00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:01.440
And so at age 28, two years into it,

611
00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:04.040
I realized like, I don't know if this business is gonna work

612
00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:06.490
and we had the San Francisco Loma Prieta or earthquake.

613
00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:09.060
And I was like, oh my god, we have no business now.

614
00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:10.690
So I started doing something that actually,

615
00:26:10.690 --> 00:26:12.170
I would recommend everybody do.

616
00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:16.220
I mean, it's just simple about metabolizing experience.

617
00:26:16.220 --> 00:26:19.670
Every weekend I would sit down with a diary.

618
00:26:19.670 --> 00:26:20.970
Somebody had given it to me as a diary.

619
00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:22.540
I didn't use it as a diary.

620
00:26:22.540 --> 00:26:27.540
I used it as a place to understand my wisdom.

621
00:26:28.170 --> 00:26:30.030
And so I called it my wisdom book.

622
00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:33.900
And each weekend I would create like three, four,

623
00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:36.320
maybe eight different bullet points

624
00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:38.310
of what I'd learned that week.

625
00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:40.320
And often the learning was painful.

626
00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:41.923
It was things like, oh man.

627
00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:46.050
Linda (indistinct) wanted to take over our whole hotel,

628
00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:49.250
but I took a day to get back to her travel agent

629
00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:50.260
and they booked something else.

630
00:26:50.260 --> 00:26:51.870
So it's like, okay.

631
00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:55.167
Simple lesson, which is like, okay if the travel agent-

632
00:26:55.167 --> 00:26:57.160
Move quickly.
Yeah move quickly.

633
00:26:57.160 --> 00:26:58.860
I mean don't waste time.

634
00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:03.860
And so long story short is every weekend for over 30 years,

635
00:27:05.750 --> 00:27:10.750
I sat down and wrote in one of 10 wisdom books,

636
00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:16.080
and now Google docs, with what I learned that week.

637
00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:19.380
And the value in doing this is, it actually allows you

638
00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:22.110
to start to metabolize your experience.

639
00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:23.990
You could do this for a team.

640
00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:26.890
You could say once a quarter, we as a leadership team

641
00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:30.600
are gonna do a vulnerable kind of experience where we said,

642
00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:33.670
what was my biggest lesson this quarter?

643
00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:37.480
And then let each person speak up about what was the lesson?

644
00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:38.890
What did they learn from it

645
00:27:38.890 --> 00:27:41.340
and how they're gonna use it moving forward?

646
00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:43.110
Because that's a way we start

647
00:27:43.110 --> 00:27:45.170
to metabolize experience into wisdom.

648
00:27:45.170 --> 00:27:46.160
Yeah, that's amazing.

649
00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:48.390
So that's a practice that you still to this day do?

650
00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:49.658
I still do, yeah.

651
00:27:49.658 --> 00:27:51.020
I don't do it every week.

652
00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:54.080
So let me be clear because I wanna be fully honest.

653
00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:55.187
I do it once a month now.

654
00:27:55.187 --> 00:27:57.130
I don't do it every weekend anymore.

655
00:27:57.130 --> 00:27:57.963
Okay.

656
00:27:57.963 --> 00:27:59.720
But I did it every weekend for about 30 years.

657
00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:00.772
Wow.

658
00:28:00.772 --> 00:28:04.760
It conjures in my mind sort of a hybrid

659
00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:07.570
between a very directed journaling exercise

660
00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:08.410
where you're going in.

661
00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:11.210
You're not just like doing morning pages

662
00:28:11.210 --> 00:28:12.620
where you're vomiting out whatever.

663
00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:13.660
You're very intentional

664
00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:17.010
about what it is that you're focused on writing about

665
00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:19.430
matched with a practice that,

666
00:28:19.430 --> 00:28:21.950
you know I've been in recovery for a long time.

667
00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:25.200
This is sort of like a daily thing to do an inventory.

668
00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:27.220
To like check yourself a little bit.

669
00:28:27.220 --> 00:28:28.460
Like, where am I going wrong?

670
00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:30.780
How did I contribute to that thing not going well,

671
00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:31.690
et cetera.

672
00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:35.202
Which leads to a deeper level of self awareness.

673
00:28:35.202 --> 00:28:38.467
Yeah, I wish someone had told me this.

674
00:28:38.467 --> 00:28:40.310
You know, it's such a simple practice,

675
00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:42.210
but such a profound one in the sense

676
00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:46.474
of our life is meant to be examined,

677
00:28:46.474 --> 00:28:50.642
but not examined as if we did something wrong.

678
00:28:50.642 --> 00:28:52.724
Maybe we made a mistake,

679
00:28:52.724 --> 00:28:55.050
but the lesson from that mistake

680
00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:57.130
actually makes us smarter for the future

681
00:28:57.130 --> 00:28:58.290
or wiser for the future.

682
00:28:58.290 --> 00:29:02.950
And so I recommend it for most of the people

683
00:29:02.950 --> 00:29:03.880
that I work with

684
00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:08.880
and there's about six CEOs I mentor today just as friends.

685
00:29:08.930 --> 00:29:11.653
I mean, I don't do it for a living.

686
00:29:13.247 --> 00:29:15.840
And I tell them it's really simple.

687
00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:19.920
But like so many things that are simple, it's a practice.

688
00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:20.753
Yeah.

689
00:29:20.753 --> 00:29:21.586
And you have to do it,

690
00:29:21.586 --> 00:29:23.920
otherwise it won't meaning to anything.

691
00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:26.820
We should mention that the pay by hour hotel,

692
00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:31.260
fleabag motel in the Tenderloin district of San Francisco

693
00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:32.350
became the Phoenix.

694
00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:33.700
I've stayed there many times,

695
00:29:33.700 --> 00:29:38.090
such an epic hotel and such a diverse dynamic neighborhood.

696
00:29:38.090 --> 00:29:40.480
I was in San Francisco this past weekend.

697
00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:41.785
I did not stay at the Phoenix.

698
00:29:41.785 --> 00:29:45.060
I ended up staying at the Proper hotel.

699
00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:46.201
Oh yeah.

700
00:29:46.201 --> 00:29:47.540
Which is on the edge of the Tenderloin.

701
00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:50.317
The Tenderloin has really not got much better.

702
00:29:50.317 --> 00:29:51.641
(both laugh)

703
00:29:51.641 --> 00:29:52.474
You know.
I bought this place-

704
00:29:52.474 --> 00:29:53.307
Since I live in San Francisco.

705
00:29:53.307 --> 00:29:55.610
It's 35 years ago and it's actually worse

706
00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:56.859
than it was 35 years ago.

707
00:29:56.859 --> 00:29:58.290
It's one of the few neighborhoods in San Francisco

708
00:29:58.290 --> 00:30:00.397
that hasn't gentrified.

709
00:30:00.397 --> 00:30:04.610
Yeah, it's a CD neighborhood and it's...

710
00:30:04.610 --> 00:30:05.540
But there's a lot.

711
00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:08.833
It is CD and there's a lot of rich history there.

712
00:30:08.833 --> 00:30:09.685
Sure.

713
00:30:09.685 --> 00:30:11.060
And a lot of really interesting cultural things.

714
00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:13.340
I was on the board of Glide Memorial Church,

715
00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:16.100
Cecil Williams Church in the tenure line for many years.

716
00:30:16.100 --> 00:30:19.613
And just beautiful, there's a lot of humanity there.

717
00:30:19.613 --> 00:30:21.325
It's sort of like going to India.

718
00:30:21.325 --> 00:30:23.640
Yeah.
(Chip laughs)

719
00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:25.780
But the Phoenix became known

720
00:30:25.780 --> 00:30:27.670
as like the rock and roll hotel.

721
00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:29.140
Like all the bands stayed there,

722
00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:31.880
like just legends of music over the years.

723
00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:32.740
Which is pretty cool.

724
00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:35.250
Like, was that an intention going into it

725
00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:37.733
or did that grow out of (indistinct) factor-

726
00:30:37.733 --> 00:30:39.527
I was a 26 year old

727
00:30:39.527 --> 00:30:42.760
who said I wanna get into the boutique hotel business

728
00:30:42.760 --> 00:30:45.000
because it seems as if it's about to take off.

729
00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:46.920
So Ian Schrager and Bill Kimpton were

730
00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:49.080
the first two U.S. boutique hoteliers.

731
00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:50.240
I was the one right after them.

732
00:30:50.240 --> 00:30:52.420
And I was like, they're successful,

733
00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:53.810
I wanna be successful too.

734
00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:57.740
And so I said, each time we would create a hotel,

735
00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:00.650
let's imagine a magazine that defines the hotel.

736
00:31:00.650 --> 00:31:03.440
And because magazines and boutique hotels

737
00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:04.273
have something in common.

738
00:31:04.273 --> 00:31:06.530
They're niche-oriented and lifestyle oriented.

739
00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:07.750
So that first hotel of Phoenix

740
00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:09.620
was based upon Rolling Stone magazine.

741
00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:11.250
And we came up with five adjectives

742
00:31:11.250 --> 00:31:12.720
that defined Rolling Stone.

743
00:31:12.720 --> 00:31:16.590
Funky, irreverent, adventurous, cool, and young at heart.

744
00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:18.818
And everything we did in creating that first hotel

745
00:31:18.818 --> 00:31:21.320
had to come back to those five adjectives.

746
00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:22.260
And then miraculously,

747
00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:25.190
what we found was the people who fell in love with the hotel

748
00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:26.780
were people who would use those five adjectives

749
00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:28.820
to describe themselves on a good day.

750
00:31:28.820 --> 00:31:31.210
So it was like, you are where you sleep.

751
00:31:31.210 --> 00:31:32.360
So that's what a boutique hotel is.

752
00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:34.160
It's sort of like an identity refreshment.

753
00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:35.901
It's aspirational.

754
00:31:35.901 --> 00:31:37.890
And so every hotel...

755
00:31:37.890 --> 00:31:39.860
Yeah, so the first hotel was all about rock and roll bands

756
00:31:39.860 --> 00:31:41.300
because at 26, like, oh yeah,

757
00:31:41.300 --> 00:31:44.700
I'd love to have Nirvana and Pearl Jam,

758
00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:47.240
and Red Hot Chili Peppers staying here all at the same time

759
00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:49.330
on their way to becoming famous bands.

760
00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:50.163
And that happened a lot,

761
00:31:50.163 --> 00:31:52.570
those three bands actually toured a lot together.

762
00:31:53.740 --> 00:31:54.710
So that's how it started.

763
00:31:54.710 --> 00:31:57.350
And then all 52 of the boutique hotels

764
00:31:57.350 --> 00:31:59.180
were based upon different magazines in different-

765
00:31:59.180 --> 00:32:00.540
So each one had its own magazine?

766
00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:02.240
It did, or sometimes a mixture of two.

767
00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:04.002
Like there's a hotel of Italian in San Francisco.

768
00:32:04.002 --> 00:32:04.835
Yeah.

769
00:32:04.835 --> 00:32:05.668
Closed-
Stayed there too.

770
00:32:05.668 --> 00:32:06.501
in the pandemic.

771
00:32:06.501 --> 00:32:10.310
So that was dwell meets real simple.

772
00:32:10.310 --> 00:32:14.720
So modern, urban, fresh, natural, and nurturing.

773
00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:16.950
And so it was sweet in some ways

774
00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:19.770
I was like an armchair psychologist (laughs).

775
00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:20.603
By saying,

776
00:32:20.603 --> 00:32:23.860
okay, what are some of the personalities out there,

777
00:32:23.860 --> 00:32:27.620
who would like to see a hotel that's there perfect habitat.

778
00:32:27.620 --> 00:32:30.920
Yeah, well, creativity is central to this,

779
00:32:30.920 --> 00:32:32.870
a knack for marketing.

780
00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:35.670
Some foresight about where this industry was headed.

781
00:32:35.670 --> 00:32:37.320
It's hard to imagine at this point

782
00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:39.052
a world without boutique hotels.

783
00:32:39.052 --> 00:32:39.885
Yeah.

784
00:32:39.885 --> 00:32:42.410
Like no young person is staying at a Hyatt or a Hilton.

785
00:32:42.410 --> 00:32:44.518
Well, they're actually staying in Airbnb.

786
00:32:44.518 --> 00:32:45.351
Well, yeah exactly.
(Chip laughs)

787
00:32:45.351 --> 00:32:46.200
Like, yeah, right-

788
00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:48.660
Actually boutique hotels were sort of a precursor

789
00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:49.874
for Airbnb.

790
00:32:49.874 --> 00:32:50.707
Sure.

791
00:32:50.707 --> 00:32:52.298
Yeah of course, like a bespoke environment.

792
00:32:52.298 --> 00:32:55.360
And just having stayed at the Proper,

793
00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:57.733
like that chain and many others.

794
00:32:57.733 --> 00:33:00.640
Owes its legacy to this kind of legwork

795
00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:02.410
that you did many years ago.

796
00:33:02.410 --> 00:33:04.630
Yeah, if you go into almost

797
00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:07.550
any full service Hyatt or Hilton today,

798
00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:09.890
they are trying to be like a boutique hotel.

799
00:33:09.890 --> 00:33:11.540
The bar is sort of central.

800
00:33:11.540 --> 00:33:14.960
The music is definitely not the music of 20 years ago.

801
00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:18.370
The design is very different than it used to be.

802
00:33:18.370 --> 00:33:22.010
So I think what the chains realized

803
00:33:22.010 --> 00:33:24.540
and then ultimately they bought many of the boutique hotels.

804
00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:26.630
My company is part of Hyatt,

805
00:33:26.630 --> 00:33:27.790
Kimpton is part of Intercontinental.

806
00:33:27.790 --> 00:33:28.630
Yeah (indistinct) of that.

807
00:33:28.630 --> 00:33:32.700
Yeah, Marriott, Hyatt, Ian Schrager like, okay, there we go.

808
00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:36.060
The bottom line is the big hotel change realized

809
00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:38.130
that they needed to get hip

810
00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:40.950
because the customer wanted more experience.

811
00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:43.619
They didn't just want a boring, predictable experience.

812
00:33:43.619 --> 00:33:44.900
(dramatic music)

813
00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:47.490
Prophets walk among us.

814
00:33:47.490 --> 00:33:51.090
As a writer and podcaster for nearly 10 years,

815
00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:53.120
I've become more convinced than ever

816
00:33:53.120 --> 00:33:55.730
that our world is populated by scores

817
00:33:55.730 --> 00:33:58.000
of beautiful and brilliant people

818
00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.350
who have amazing stories to share.

819
00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:03.590
Those that we don't know who can teach us something new

820
00:34:03.590 --> 00:34:06.000
and leave us all the better for the experience

821
00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:07.550
of their sharing.

822
00:34:07.550 --> 00:34:09.890
And so I've dedicated my career to tracking down

823
00:34:09.890 --> 00:34:12.360
the most compelling prophets on the planet.

824
00:34:12.360 --> 00:34:15.410
Going deep with each of them on my podcast

825
00:34:15.410 --> 00:34:18.270
to elucidate the best of what they have to offer,

826
00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:22.650
and to sharing the insights gleaned for the benefit of all.

827
00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.660
But the podcast is not the only medium

828
00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:26.850
by which to share their stories.

829
00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:28.580
Which is why I'm proud to announce

830
00:34:28.580 --> 00:34:32.960
the release of my new book, "Voicing Change Volume II."

831
00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:35.117
More than mere words on paper,

832
00:34:35.117 --> 00:34:38.477
"Voicing Change" is a physical manifestation of the magic,

833
00:34:38.477 --> 00:34:42.790
inspiration, and timeless wisdom that transpires

834
00:34:42.790 --> 00:34:45.469
each week on the Rich Roll podcast.

835
00:34:45.469 --> 00:34:47.870
The first edition of "Voicing Change,"

836
00:34:47.870 --> 00:34:50.510
was a beautifully rendered book worthy of display

837
00:34:50.510 --> 00:34:52.183
on any coffee table.

838
00:34:52.183 --> 00:34:55.450
And volume two follows in that tradition

839
00:34:55.450 --> 00:34:59.060
by showcasing even more of my favorite conversations

840
00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:01.080
in an elegant publication,

841
00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:04.480
replete with interview excerpts, essays,

842
00:35:04.480 --> 00:35:06.170
and stunning photography.

843
00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:09.615
Making bur exquisite companion to the first volume

844
00:35:09.615 --> 00:35:12.932
or a satisfying standalone work.

845
00:35:12.932 --> 00:35:16.430
Picking up this book allows you to revisit the wisdom

846
00:35:16.430 --> 00:35:18.666
of your favorite everyday prophets,

847
00:35:18.666 --> 00:35:21.930
and physically interact with the life changing ideas

848
00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:23.043
contained with them.

849
00:35:24.447 --> 00:35:26.635
"Voicing Change Volume II," available now

850
00:35:26.635 --> 00:35:30.030
while supplies last for a limited time.

851
00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:34.100
Order your copy today only at richroll.com

852
00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:38.610
Back to this idea of creativity.

853
00:35:38.610 --> 00:35:41.630
It's interesting that out of business school,

854
00:35:41.630 --> 00:35:44.880
your initial kind of career path was commercial real estate,

855
00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:47.820
not exactly known for creative inspiration, right?

856
00:35:47.820 --> 00:35:51.160
Just buying office space and leasing it, et cetera.

857
00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:54.920
So how did you reconcile that creative spark

858
00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:57.020
that I'm sure you had some self-awareness around,

859
00:35:57.020 --> 00:36:00.473
and maybe a latent desire to be an entrepreneur

860
00:36:00.473 --> 00:36:02.600
with being in that track

861
00:36:02.600 --> 00:36:04.700
and then ultimately getting to the point

862
00:36:04.700 --> 00:36:07.310
where you were capable of manifesting it.

863
00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:09.070
So it's funny between my first

864
00:36:09.070 --> 00:36:10.440
and second year of business school,

865
00:36:10.440 --> 00:36:11.360
I worked in New York

866
00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:13.652
for Morgan Stanley's real estate division.

867
00:36:13.652 --> 00:36:16.300
It's so hard to just visualizing you (laughs).

868
00:36:16.300 --> 00:36:17.233
I know.

869
00:36:17.233 --> 00:36:18.880
But sort of classic,

870
00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:21.228
like you're a business school student.

871
00:36:21.228 --> 00:36:23.090
I mean, I was 22 between my first and second year

872
00:36:23.090 --> 00:36:23.923
of business school.

873
00:36:23.923 --> 00:36:26.283
The fact I was wearing suspenders smoking cigars,

874
00:36:26.283 --> 00:36:29.291
and making deals as a Morgan Stanley real estate guy,

875
00:36:29.291 --> 00:36:30.850
was sort of beyond me.

876
00:36:30.850 --> 00:36:34.208
And they loved me and that gave me some great job offers

877
00:36:34.208 --> 00:36:36.130
after business school.

878
00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:37.201
But I just knew...

879
00:36:37.201 --> 00:36:40.060
I think the question I had in my mind was,

880
00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:41.820
what do I want to be when I grow up?

881
00:36:41.820 --> 00:36:43.840
And what's gonna take me there?

882
00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:45.620
Because actually the amount of money

883
00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:46.510
they were throwing at me,

884
00:36:46.510 --> 00:36:50.030
and the amount of prestige they were gonna give me,

885
00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:52.410
they even knew that I wanted to be maybe back

886
00:36:52.410 --> 00:36:54.290
in the West Coast sometimes soon.

887
00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:56.370
So they're gonna like bring me to New York for a year

888
00:36:56.370 --> 00:36:59.830
and then help run and open the office in LA,

889
00:36:59.830 --> 00:37:01.075
the real estate office there.

890
00:37:01.075 --> 00:37:04.523
I was like, you don't do this for a 22 or 23-year-old.

891
00:37:05.710 --> 00:37:06.910
But I turned it down.

892
00:37:06.910 --> 00:37:08.400
I turned down all the other job offers.

893
00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:11.467
I took a job out of business school for $2,000 a month.

894
00:37:11.467 --> 00:37:14.960
$24,000 a year, which was crazy.

895
00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:17.680
I mean the average salary out of business school

896
00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:20.740
at that point was about 85 to $90,000 a year.

897
00:37:20.740 --> 00:37:23.610
But I took it because it was working for a creative...

898
00:37:23.610 --> 00:37:24.840
What I thought was a creative

899
00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:27.686
real estate development company that was...

900
00:37:27.686 --> 00:37:31.060
Basically redeveloping what was called showplace square,

901
00:37:31.060 --> 00:37:32.210
which was south of market.

902
00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:35.440
And I just thought, you know what, I'm gonna go work there.

903
00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:38.650
And the guy who ran the show and founded it,

904
00:37:38.650 --> 00:37:40.700
said you could become a partner within a year.

905
00:37:40.700 --> 00:37:45.050
And so I went to work there, but it wasn't creative enough.

906
00:37:45.050 --> 00:37:49.590
I mean, I thought I chose creativity over money

907
00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:52.370
and even the creative real estate development thing

908
00:37:52.370 --> 00:37:55.110
I went to do wasn't creative enough for me.

909
00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:58.700
So that's when I get to know again Bill Graham.

910
00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:00.144
Bill Graham, the concert promoter.

911
00:38:00.144 --> 00:38:00.977
Sure.

912
00:38:00.977 --> 00:38:02.730
So Bill Graham was building shoreline and amphitheater

913
00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:06.400
down in Mountain View on the peninsula, in the Bay Area.

914
00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:10.320
And our CEO founder wanted me to do a deal

915
00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:12.172
with Bill Graham (laughs).

916
00:38:12.172 --> 00:38:13.440
So we were gonna be his partner on shoreline.

917
00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:16.540
After that theater, I was like, okay, well, good luck.

918
00:38:16.540 --> 00:38:19.490
I mean, Graham's got the money, he knows how to develop it.

919
00:38:19.490 --> 00:38:21.240
What's our part in this thing.

920
00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:23.000
So ultimately there was no deal to be made.

921
00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:25.030
But Bill Graham pulled me aside,

922
00:38:25.030 --> 00:38:26.470
he said, "I like you sonny."

923
00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:27.380
He says...

924
00:38:27.380 --> 00:38:29.014
At this point I was 25.

925
00:38:29.014 --> 00:38:30.497
"I like your head spot.

926
00:38:30.497 --> 00:38:33.007
"I like you're mensch.

927
00:38:33.007 --> 00:38:34.557
"You know what you really need to do?

928
00:38:34.557 --> 00:38:37.747
"Is you really need to create a hotel in San Francisco

929
00:38:37.747 --> 00:38:39.220
"for the rock and roll bands."

930
00:38:39.220 --> 00:38:40.053
Wow.

931
00:38:40.053 --> 00:38:40.930
Because I have people coming to the war field

932
00:38:40.930 --> 00:38:42.200
and the Fillmore and they're coming

933
00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:44.030
to Great American Musical Hall

934
00:38:44.030 --> 00:38:47.240
and the only place they go is an old place

935
00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:49.580
called the Miyako, that was in Japantown,

936
00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:51.160
which ultimately became the Hotel Kabuki,

937
00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:53.090
which is Joie de Vivre hotel.

938
00:38:53.090 --> 00:38:57.440
And they said there's no great place for them to stay.

939
00:38:57.440 --> 00:38:59.510
And that's when the light bulb wheels

940
00:38:59.510 --> 00:39:01.780
started to turn over my head.

941
00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:04.430
And I was like, okay, maybe that's what I should do.

942
00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:07.480
So I went to the head founder of our firm and I said,

943
00:39:07.480 --> 00:39:09.470
I have a really creative idea for you.

944
00:39:09.470 --> 00:39:12.290
And I told him what Bill Graham said to me

945
00:39:12.290 --> 00:39:13.123
and he was like,

946
00:39:13.123 --> 00:39:14.907
"Dude, we're not gonna get in the hotel business.

947
00:39:14.907 --> 00:39:16.197
"Like, we'll lose our shirts.

948
00:39:16.197 --> 00:39:17.330
"It's a terrible business."

949
00:39:17.330 --> 00:39:18.933
Yeah, that's the thing with hotels.

950
00:39:18.933 --> 00:39:21.520
Like if you wanna lose a ton of money, get into hotels.

951
00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:22.353
Yeah, exactly.

952
00:39:22.353 --> 00:39:24.930
Same with restaurants it's a sort of...

953
00:39:24.930 --> 00:39:27.892
But I just said, you know what, I'm gonna go do it.

954
00:39:27.892 --> 00:39:29.230
And so I started this company

955
00:39:29.230 --> 00:39:34.230
on my 26th birthday and and bought the motel

956
00:39:34.330 --> 00:39:37.100
on a 40-year land lease for $800,000.

957
00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:40.270
Can you imagine buying a 44-room property

958
00:39:40.270 --> 00:39:43.010
on an acre of land in San Francisco for $800,000.

959
00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:45.580
You can't buy a condo for $800,000 in San Francisco,

960
00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:46.413
A garage.

961
00:39:46.413 --> 00:39:48.520
And that's what I bought.

962
00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:50.650
And here we are 36 years later,

963
00:39:50.650 --> 00:39:54.010
and I still own the Phoenix.

964
00:39:54.010 --> 00:39:55.430
Although we only have about two and a half,

965
00:39:55.430 --> 00:39:57.270
three years left on the land leases.

966
00:39:57.270 --> 00:39:58.420
So how did you...

967
00:39:58.420 --> 00:40:01.440
That story aside, like how did you transcend

968
00:40:01.440 --> 00:40:04.660
that kind of conventional wisdom around hotels

969
00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:05.540
are the way to lose money.

970
00:40:05.540 --> 00:40:09.100
I'm thinking a scene in one of the "Ocean's 11" movies

971
00:40:09.100 --> 00:40:11.520
where Brad Pitt, like it appears

972
00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:13.720
that he bought the standard hotel on sunset.

973
00:40:13.720 --> 00:40:14.553
Yes.

974
00:40:14.553 --> 00:40:16.230
And he's like hemorrhaging all the money

975
00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:17.880
that he had stolen in the earlier movie.

976
00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:19.250
And everyone's making fun of him.

977
00:40:19.250 --> 00:40:20.083
That's so funny.

978
00:40:20.083 --> 00:40:24.140
That particular property before it was bought

979
00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:27.950
by Andre Balazs and made it into the standard.

980
00:40:27.950 --> 00:40:29.330
I had it in escrow.

981
00:40:29.330 --> 00:40:31.760
But it was an old Jewish convalescent home.

982
00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:32.760
And I'll never forget.

983
00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:35.680
So this is now we're getting in some good stories.

984
00:40:35.680 --> 00:40:36.513
Yes.

985
00:40:36.513 --> 00:40:38.420
I walked through it after we were in escrow,

986
00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:40.690
and this older Jewish woman came up to me

987
00:40:40.690 --> 00:40:42.730
and pulled on my shirt sleeve.

988
00:40:42.730 --> 00:40:43.997
And she says,

989
00:40:43.997 --> 00:40:46.217
"Are you the guy who's gonna be actually buying this

990
00:40:46.217 --> 00:40:48.140
"and throwing us out?"

991
00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:49.315
Oh no!

992
00:40:49.315 --> 00:40:52.527
And I just had this crisis of conscience and I said,

993
00:40:52.527 --> 00:40:53.530
"Not gonna do that."

994
00:40:53.530 --> 00:40:56.470
We'll let Brad Pitt do it.

995
00:40:56.470 --> 00:40:57.649
We'll let Brad Pitt do exactly.

996
00:40:57.649 --> 00:41:01.697
I think what made us different,

997
00:41:01.697 --> 00:41:05.570
and the reason we succeeded is we got clear

998
00:41:05.570 --> 00:41:08.240
that the reason a person chooses a boutique hotel

999
00:41:08.240 --> 00:41:11.910
is because there's an aspirational lifestyle piece to it.

1000
00:41:11.910 --> 00:41:14.410
Now, Ian Schrager, who was really one of the first

1001
00:41:14.410 --> 00:41:16.190
he and Bill Kimp really were the two first

1002
00:41:16.190 --> 00:41:17.129
boutique hoteliers.

1003
00:41:17.129 --> 00:41:19.080
They had different perspectives.

1004
00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:22.150
So Schrager's perspective was it's all about being cool,

1005
00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:23.600
and maybe even narcissistic.

1006
00:41:23.600 --> 00:41:28.600
And so all of his hotels were about getting behind

1007
00:41:29.040 --> 00:41:29.873
the velvet rope.

1008
00:41:29.873 --> 00:41:30.706
Sure.

1009
00:41:30.706 --> 00:41:34.323
And Kimptons was much more sort of home spun European,

1010
00:41:35.440 --> 00:41:37.470
more like an in kind of experience,

1011
00:41:37.470 --> 00:41:40.573
where you have a good restaurant there, and a wine hour.

1012
00:41:41.410 --> 00:41:43.830
But they sort of kept playing the same game

1013
00:41:43.830 --> 00:41:44.840
over and over again.

1014
00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:47.240
And my point of view was like, geez,

1015
00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:50.100
there's so many different kinds of personalities out there.

1016
00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:52.500
Let's have this very schizophrenic company

1017
00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:54.270
where we have 52 different hotels

1018
00:41:54.270 --> 00:41:56.330
with all kinds of different personalities

1019
00:41:56.330 --> 00:41:57.650
that we're actually serving.

1020
00:41:57.650 --> 00:41:58.729
And so that's how-

1021
00:41:58.729 --> 00:41:59.870
Right.

1022
00:41:59.870 --> 00:42:02.220
But that's riskier because some of those aren't gonna work.

1023
00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:03.053
It's very riskier.

1024
00:42:03.053 --> 00:42:05.030
It's riskier and in some ways it's stupid

1025
00:42:05.030 --> 00:42:07.190
because we were like a branding agency.

1026
00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:09.250
Every single time we were creating a new hotel,

1027
00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:12.240
it was a new brand, a new market we were going after.

1028
00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:13.660
But it was very creative.

1029
00:42:13.660 --> 00:42:17.380
It was so much fun to create a hotel based upon Vanity Fair

1030
00:42:17.380 --> 00:42:20.592
meets Outside Magazine.

1031
00:42:20.592 --> 00:42:24.620
Which was a place called Costanoa, a luxury campground.

1032
00:42:24.620 --> 00:42:28.060
And so I loved it until I hated it.

1033
00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:29.290
So can I talk about that story now?

1034
00:42:29.290 --> 00:42:30.290
Yeah, let's talk about that.

1035
00:42:30.290 --> 00:42:32.610
Oh, (indistinct) Rich.

1036
00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:34.833
I was so, oh man.

1037
00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:39.580
So first of all, let's go back to, I was 26.

1038
00:42:39.580 --> 00:42:41.410
So one of the things we haven't talked about yet is,

1039
00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:43.430
so I played water polo at Stanford.

1040
00:42:43.430 --> 00:42:45.372
I was in a fraternity at Stanford.

1041
00:42:45.372 --> 00:42:47.640
I was Steven Townson Conley junior.

1042
00:42:47.640 --> 00:42:50.530
My dad was Stevens senior.

1043
00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:54.469
My dad was a Marine captain in the reserves.

1044
00:42:54.469 --> 00:42:56.490
I went to the same high school as my dad.

1045
00:42:56.490 --> 00:42:58.280
My dad was my baseball coach.

1046
00:42:58.280 --> 00:42:59.450
I was the star pitcher.

1047
00:42:59.450 --> 00:43:02.560
