1301
00:53:34.710 --> 00:53:36.420
that's the first truth of Buddhism

1302
00:53:36.420 --> 00:53:38.890
is suffering is ever present.

1303
00:53:38.890 --> 00:53:42.940
Then despair and meaning are inversely proportional.

1304
00:53:42.940 --> 00:53:46.530
So when you're going through a really crappy time,

1305
00:53:46.530 --> 00:53:50.120
the key is to ask yourself, what's the meaning.

1306
00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:52.110
And it really did take me 20 years back

1307
00:53:52.110 --> 00:53:55.440
to when I said, okay, my wisdom book

1308
00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:56.690
what did I learn this week?

1309
00:53:56.690 --> 00:54:00.860
I got very fixated on what am I learning?

1310
00:54:00.860 --> 00:54:01.693
What am I learning?

1311
00:54:01.693 --> 00:54:03.610
And what's the meaning in this?

1312
00:54:03.610 --> 00:54:05.780
And ultimately within two years, less than two years,

1313
00:54:05.780 --> 00:54:09.020
I'd sold the company at the bottom of the Great Recession.

1314
00:54:09.020 --> 00:54:12.080
I had ended the romantic relationship

1315
00:54:12.080 --> 00:54:14.330
I'd been in for a long time that wasn't working.

1316
00:54:14.330 --> 00:54:17.070
I'd gotten my foster son out of prison,

1317
00:54:17.070 --> 00:54:21.200
out of San Quentin because a federal judge let him out

1318
00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:23.460
and said, he's not guilty.

1319
00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:26.259
He's constitutional rights were abridged.

1320
00:54:26.259 --> 00:54:27.092
Wow.

1321
00:54:27.092 --> 00:54:32.092
And I was on a path to saying, I'm ready for the magic.

1322
00:54:32.290 --> 00:54:34.630
I mean, I'm ready to actually create space in my life

1323
00:54:34.630 --> 00:54:35.600
and see what emerges.

1324
00:54:35.600 --> 00:54:38.070
Because I've spent the last two dozen years

1325
00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:39.930
building this identity.

1326
00:54:39.930 --> 00:54:42.160
And it's not a facade.

1327
00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:45.460
I mean, the whole time I was living my truth,

1328
00:54:45.460 --> 00:54:50.460
but I was also getting to a place where I was so fixated

1329
00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:53.740
with the identity that I wasn't actually giving space

1330
00:54:53.740 --> 00:54:56.760
for the new creativity to come in.

1331
00:54:56.760 --> 00:54:58.070
And so, yeah.

1332
00:54:58.070 --> 00:54:59.990
And that it was not long after that,

1333
00:54:59.990 --> 00:55:01.550
that Airbnb came calling.

1334
00:55:01.550 --> 00:55:05.480
So in the midst of experiencing that level of despair

1335
00:55:05.480 --> 00:55:07.170
and the self-awareness that you had

1336
00:55:07.170 --> 00:55:10.380
to find or attach some meaning to it,

1337
00:55:10.380 --> 00:55:13.270
walk me through the process of how you identified that.

1338
00:55:13.270 --> 00:55:15.640
Because I think there's probably a lot of people

1339
00:55:15.640 --> 00:55:18.180
who are experiencing some level of despair.

1340
00:55:18.180 --> 00:55:20.330
It's been a really hard couple years.

1341
00:55:20.330 --> 00:55:25.330
Is the idea that there is universal meaning in all suffering

1342
00:55:26.290 --> 00:55:29.850
or is it that you have to find a personal connection,

1343
00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:31.360
like your own definition

1344
00:55:31.360 --> 00:55:34.370
of how it could be meaningful for you.

1345
00:55:34.370 --> 00:55:35.982
Well, let's use Frankl for a minute.

1346
00:55:35.982 --> 00:55:40.982
Viktor Frankl and folks were in the concentration camp.

1347
00:55:42.071 --> 00:55:46.120
What he says in his book is that when he saw people

1348
00:55:46.120 --> 00:55:51.120
having hope and had a sense of the other side of this,

1349
00:55:52.748 --> 00:55:54.602
not false hope.

1350
00:55:54.602 --> 00:55:58.780
The people who actually died quickly,

1351
00:55:58.780 --> 00:56:00.570
were the ones who said by this date,

1352
00:56:00.570 --> 00:56:02.180
we are gonna be outta here.

1353
00:56:02.180 --> 00:56:03.170
And that was a false hope,

1354
00:56:03.170 --> 00:56:06.180
because they had no control over them being released

1355
00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:07.600
by that date.

1356
00:56:07.600 --> 00:56:10.410
But there was more of a focus on hope

1357
00:56:10.410 --> 00:56:12.170
for what the future will be,

1358
00:56:12.170 --> 00:56:16.590
and how this experience will actually create

1359
00:56:16.590 --> 00:56:18.750
maybe a better life for them.

1360
00:56:18.750 --> 00:56:22.360
And so that's not a bad way to look at it,

1361
00:56:22.360 --> 00:56:24.750
because what we do know is that when we're going through

1362
00:56:24.750 --> 00:56:25.960
difficult times.

1363
00:56:25.960 --> 00:56:28.190
It is exceptionally character building.

1364
00:56:28.190 --> 00:56:29.980
It can be not for everybody.

1365
00:56:29.980 --> 00:56:31.084
Sometimes people give up.

1366
00:56:31.084 --> 00:56:33.160
Sometimes people do whatever they do

1367
00:56:33.160 --> 00:56:36.940
and where they drink, they use distractions.

1368
00:56:36.940 --> 00:56:39.610
They use alcohol, they use drugs, they use porn,

1369
00:56:39.610 --> 00:56:41.450
they use whatever it is to distract themselves

1370
00:56:41.450 --> 00:56:43.410
from the learning.

1371
00:56:43.410 --> 00:56:46.760
There is work sometimes as well.

1372
00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:51.760
So I think the key for me was to ask myself,

1373
00:56:52.598 --> 00:56:55.663
how is my character?

1374
00:56:56.830 --> 00:57:00.793
This may not be a resume builder, surviving a downturn.

1375
00:57:02.320 --> 00:57:03.710
So how do we...

1376
00:57:03.710 --> 00:57:05.730
You know to use some David Brooks thinking,

1377
00:57:05.730 --> 00:57:09.710
how do we move from the resume values to the eulogy values?

1378
00:57:09.710 --> 00:57:11.810
And the eulogy values are the values that people

1379
00:57:11.810 --> 00:57:13.860
will say about you at your funeral.

1380
00:57:13.860 --> 00:57:15.970
And they're the character qualities.

1381
00:57:15.970 --> 00:57:19.760
And I think it was around that age in my late 40s

1382
00:57:19.760 --> 00:57:22.923
that I realized I'd spent my life building a resume.

1383
00:57:24.150 --> 00:57:26.060
And I'd built some character qualities along the way,

1384
00:57:26.060 --> 00:57:28.330
but I was never really focused on

1385
00:57:28.330 --> 00:57:30.515
how am I developing those character qualities.

1386
00:57:30.515 --> 00:57:35.460
And if you want a time to develop your character qualities,

1387
00:57:35.460 --> 00:57:37.280
use the dark time to do that.

1388
00:57:37.280 --> 00:57:39.886
Because it's an exceptional time.

1389
00:57:39.886 --> 00:57:40.719
Sure.

1390
00:57:40.719 --> 00:57:43.600
And it's time where you actually often have to break habits.

1391
00:57:43.600 --> 00:57:46.948
Yeah, I have my own experience with that.

1392
00:57:46.948 --> 00:57:47.990
Yeah.

1393
00:57:47.990 --> 00:57:49.281
Tell us.

1394
00:57:49.281 --> 00:57:50.114
It's painful.

1395
00:57:50.114 --> 00:57:51.090
Well, I mean, I'm not gonna bore everyone

1396
00:57:51.090 --> 00:57:52.000
who's listening to this-

1397
00:57:52.000 --> 00:57:52.890
They're listening.

1398
00:57:52.890 --> 00:57:55.243
They're on this show because they (indistinct) you.

1399
00:57:55.243 --> 00:57:58.960
But I mean I had a version of that reckoning at 40,

1400
00:57:58.960 --> 00:58:01.160
and it was sort of a collision of a health scare

1401
00:58:01.160 --> 00:58:04.830
with an existential crisis about how I was living my life.

1402
00:58:04.830 --> 00:58:07.280
I was a corporate lawyer at the time and very unhappy,

1403
00:58:07.280 --> 00:58:10.480
but also very much in the checking the boxes

1404
00:58:10.480 --> 00:58:13.810
and trying to make sure that I was the dutiful son

1405
00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:17.100
to parents with high expectations, et cetera.

1406
00:58:17.100 --> 00:58:19.365
Something that you know quite well.

1407
00:58:19.365 --> 00:58:22.670
And had to really deconstruct all of that

1408
00:58:22.670 --> 00:58:24.240
and figure out who I wanted to be.

1409
00:58:24.240 --> 00:58:26.530
And then spent a decade trying to figure that out.

1410
00:58:26.530 --> 00:58:28.240
I'm still trying to figure it out.

1411
00:58:28.240 --> 00:58:32.290
And I found a modality that I leveraged

1412
00:58:32.290 --> 00:58:34.970
was endurance sports and all of the kind of quiet time

1413
00:58:34.970 --> 00:58:36.400
that comes with that,

1414
00:58:36.400 --> 00:58:40.420
that allowed me to develop a level of self connection

1415
00:58:40.420 --> 00:58:42.592
that I was lacking at the time.

1416
00:58:42.592 --> 00:58:43.714
And ultimately leading me

1417
00:58:43.714 --> 00:58:48.714
kind of into a more intuition-based, heart-centered place

1418
00:58:50.300 --> 00:58:52.020
to be open to what was next.

1419
00:58:52.020 --> 00:58:53.700
And Airbnb didn't call,

1420
00:58:53.700 --> 00:58:56.410
but slowly other opportunities came.

1421
00:58:56.410 --> 00:58:58.629
And that's kind of why I'm sitting here today.

1422
00:58:58.629 --> 00:59:03.629
And that evolution and having it be on display for others

1423
00:59:05.826 --> 00:59:10.826
has been incredibly cathartic to people to see your process.

1424
00:59:12.760 --> 00:59:16.300
Especially you're from 40 to 55, that is a...

1425
00:59:16.300 --> 00:59:18.530
I would say your curve of happiness

1426
00:59:18.530 --> 00:59:20.820
shows that 47.2 is the low point.

1427
00:59:20.820 --> 00:59:22.790
Again, your mileage may vary.

1428
00:59:22.790 --> 00:59:25.623
But from 40 to 55 a lot's going on.

1429
00:59:26.610 --> 00:59:28.980
I use another emotional equation.

1430
00:59:28.980 --> 00:59:32.250
Disappointment equals expectations minus reality.

1431
00:59:32.250 --> 00:59:33.210
So disappointment equals...

1432
00:59:33.210 --> 00:59:37.110
So it's around 40 to 50, 55

1433
00:59:37.110 --> 00:59:39.780
that you start to come face-to-face with the expectations

1434
00:59:39.780 --> 00:59:42.480
that you have for yourself.

1435
00:59:42.480 --> 00:59:44.620
The perfect spouse,

1436
00:59:44.620 --> 00:59:47.070
that you're supposed to be president of the United States.

1437
00:59:47.070 --> 00:59:48.570
How much wealth you're supposed to have.

1438
00:59:48.570 --> 00:59:50.110
The second home you're supposed to have.

1439
00:59:50.110 --> 00:59:52.000
The kids who are perfect.

1440
00:59:52.000 --> 00:59:55.040
And in your 40s, you come face-to-face with the fact

1441
00:59:55.040 --> 00:59:57.300
that none of that's actually happening.

1442
00:59:57.300 --> 00:59:58.590
And you also may come face-to-face

1443
00:59:58.590 --> 01:00:02.700
with the fact that this success script that you were handed

1444
01:00:02.700 --> 01:00:06.730
by your parents is not the script

1445
01:00:06.730 --> 01:00:08.500
that you wanna write for yourself.

1446
01:00:08.500 --> 01:00:10.730
And is not leading to the fulfillment

1447
01:00:10.730 --> 01:00:12.510
that was implicitly promised.

1448
01:00:12.510 --> 01:00:13.920
That's right.

1449
01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:18.730
And so lucky for me at 22 I came out of the closet

1450
01:00:18.730 --> 01:00:21.440
because it forced me to actually come face-to-face

1451
01:00:21.440 --> 01:00:25.351
with my success script from my parents.

1452
01:00:25.351 --> 01:00:26.820
And my lovely parents.

1453
01:00:26.820 --> 01:00:27.653
I love my parents

1454
01:00:27.653 --> 01:00:31.670
and they've been after rough, initial time,

1455
01:00:31.670 --> 01:00:33.670
they've been incredibly supportive and loving.

1456
01:00:33.670 --> 01:00:36.140
And love the fact that they're on a six week vacation

1457
01:00:36.140 --> 01:00:38.319
right now at age 84.

1458
01:00:38.319 --> 01:00:42.840
But what I can say is that being able to deconstruct

1459
01:00:42.840 --> 01:00:44.260
the success script,

1460
01:00:44.260 --> 01:00:47.000
in such a way that you recognize

1461
01:00:47.000 --> 01:00:49.790
that you're the screenwriter of your own life,

1462
01:00:49.790 --> 01:00:53.340
and you need sometimes some guidance

1463
01:00:53.340 --> 01:00:55.463
for how to write that screenplay.

1464
01:00:56.460 --> 01:00:59.330
Partly because you may have constructed your life

1465
01:00:59.330 --> 01:01:00.680
in a certain way.

1466
01:01:00.680 --> 01:01:02.280
And it's not just like, oh, okay,

1467
01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:04.030
now I'm gonna go do this other thing.

1468
01:01:04.030 --> 01:01:05.659
No, you actually have three kids

1469
01:01:05.659 --> 01:01:09.510
and you get a spouse and you've got a mortgage,

1470
01:01:09.510 --> 01:01:12.360
and you're actually helping your ailing parents.

1471
01:01:12.360 --> 01:01:14.360
At the same time you're helping your teenagers.

1472
01:01:14.360 --> 01:01:16.890
So you've got the sandwich generation going on.

1473
01:01:16.890 --> 01:01:19.160
And so the process of doing that...

1474
01:01:19.160 --> 01:01:20.480
I witnessed my father.

1475
01:01:20.480 --> 01:01:25.480
My father who dutifully became a banker like his father

1476
01:01:26.313 --> 01:01:31.050
around his early 40s, said you know, fuck this.

1477
01:01:31.050 --> 01:01:32.090
I don't wanna do this.

1478
01:01:32.090 --> 01:01:37.090
And he created a business when he had me at Stanford,

1479
01:01:37.410 --> 01:01:39.200
my sister about to go to Stanford,

1480
01:01:39.200 --> 01:01:41.910
and then another sister gonna go to UC Berkeley

1481
01:01:41.910 --> 01:01:43.420
a few years later.

1482
01:01:43.420 --> 01:01:45.120
And he started a company at a time

1483
01:01:45.120 --> 01:01:47.210
where it was really not opportune.

1484
01:01:47.210 --> 01:01:50.300
So my father's been a role model for me in many ways.

1485
01:01:50.300 --> 01:01:51.762
It's interesting.

1486
01:01:51.762 --> 01:01:54.910
And it is around our 40s and 50s,

1487
01:01:54.910 --> 01:01:57.050
and some people's reaction to all that is,

1488
01:01:57.050 --> 01:01:59.026
go get the red Ferrari.

1489
01:01:59.026 --> 01:02:00.890
Or have the affair.

1490
01:02:00.890 --> 01:02:05.890
And I mean, often that's just another form of a distraction.

1491
01:02:07.440 --> 01:02:10.650
Yeah, I want to get into the mentorship piece.

1492
01:02:10.650 --> 01:02:12.640
But before that, I'm not letting you off the hook

1493
01:02:12.640 --> 01:02:14.007
on that near death experience.

1494
01:02:14.007 --> 01:02:14.840
Oh, yeah.

1495
01:02:14.840 --> 01:02:17.790
I want to hear what that was like specifically.

1496
01:02:17.790 --> 01:02:20.750
Well, since it happened nine times in 90 minutes,

1497
01:02:20.750 --> 01:02:24.900
and I had one particular nurse there by my side,

1498
01:02:24.900 --> 01:02:26.220
four of those nine times,

1499
01:02:26.220 --> 01:02:28.970
and each time I would come out of my NDE,

1500
01:02:28.970 --> 01:02:30.550
I would say to her, here's what I saw.

1501
01:02:30.550 --> 01:02:32.770
And she would say to me, "That's what you said last time."

1502
01:02:32.770 --> 01:02:35.220
And the last time was only about six minutes ago.

1503
01:02:36.700 --> 01:02:39.200
So here's what I saw.

1504
01:02:39.200 --> 01:02:44.200
And I've hung out with a lot of NDE folks.

1505
01:02:44.500 --> 01:02:45.610
There's even a...

1506
01:02:45.610 --> 01:02:46.930
I'm fascinated by festivals

1507
01:02:46.930 --> 01:02:49.740
because I've a founding board member

1508
01:02:49.740 --> 01:02:51.810
of the Burning Man Nonprofit.

1509
01:02:51.810 --> 01:02:56.450
And there's a festival in Northwestern Spain

1510
01:02:56.450 --> 01:02:59.640
called the Pilgrimage of the Near Death Experience.

1511
01:02:59.640 --> 01:03:04.340
And everybody who had death experience is put in a coffin

1512
01:03:04.340 --> 01:03:06.750
that's open and you have a parade

1513
01:03:06.750 --> 01:03:08.310
of coffins going down the street.

1514
01:03:08.310 --> 01:03:10.677
My you're waving in everybody, and everybody's drinking

1515
01:03:10.677 --> 01:03:13.130
and it sounds sort of weird and macabre.

1516
01:03:13.130 --> 01:03:16.273
So my experience was this,

1517
01:03:20.140 --> 01:03:21.980
the first thing I noticed was

1518
01:03:21.980 --> 01:03:24.240
that I was in a mountain chalet.

1519
01:03:24.240 --> 01:03:27.235
So maybe let's say eight or 10,000 feet

1520
01:03:27.235 --> 01:03:31.005
in some mountain range.

1521
01:03:31.005 --> 01:03:33.590
It was like maybe a summer day.

1522
01:03:33.590 --> 01:03:36.377
I didn't notice any snow.

1523
01:03:36.377 --> 01:03:39.390
But there was this huge skylight in the living room,

1524
01:03:39.390 --> 01:03:40.610
the upstairs living room.

1525
01:03:40.610 --> 01:03:45.610
And the skylight was open and there was light coming in,

1526
01:03:45.960 --> 01:03:49.570
and there was a kaleidoscope of colors

1527
01:03:49.570 --> 01:03:54.570
that was actually being projected onto the wall.

1528
01:03:54.680 --> 01:03:57.440
I was about maybe eight or 10 feet in the air,

1529
01:03:57.440 --> 01:03:58.520
just sort of floating.

1530
01:03:58.520 --> 01:04:00.210
I didn't see myself, I didn't observe myself.

1531
01:04:00.210 --> 01:04:02.080
I just knew I was not on the ground.

1532
01:04:02.080 --> 01:04:04.879
I was sort of observing from that place.

1533
01:04:04.879 --> 01:04:08.020
There were birds chirping.

1534
01:04:08.020 --> 01:04:08.853
It was gorgeous.

1535
01:04:08.853 --> 01:04:10.740
I mean, it was sort of quite beautiful.

1536
01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:13.240
And then the thing that was most noticeable

1537
01:04:13.240 --> 01:04:16.220
was on the ground was this beautiful wood floor.

1538
01:04:16.220 --> 01:04:17.053
And on the ground,

1539
01:04:17.053 --> 01:04:19.870
there was this frangipani scented.

1540
01:04:19.870 --> 01:04:23.980
So like a tropically scented oil that was very thick,

1541
01:04:23.980 --> 01:04:25.470
like a viscous oil.

1542
01:04:25.470 --> 01:04:27.170
That was actually made.

1543
01:04:27.170 --> 01:04:29.110
It created a sheen on the floor.

1544
01:04:29.110 --> 01:04:34.110
And the oil was going toward the staircase going downstairs.

1545
01:04:34.510 --> 01:04:38.630
But it's sort of like that Heinz 57 commercial anticipation.

1546
01:04:38.630 --> 01:04:42.900
It's like the oil was dripping down the stairs

1547
01:04:42.900 --> 01:04:44.503
exceptionally slowly.

1548
01:04:45.950 --> 01:04:49.251
So what I noticed more than anything of around this,

1549
01:04:49.251 --> 01:04:51.900
there's no other people, there was no white light,

1550
01:04:51.900 --> 01:04:55.020
other than this light coming from the skylight.

1551
01:04:55.020 --> 01:04:56.230
Which was like a white light

1552
01:04:56.230 --> 01:04:59.350
that led to the kaleidoscope of colors on the wall.

1553
01:04:59.350 --> 01:05:01.840
What I noticed is how sensual it all was,

1554
01:05:01.840 --> 01:05:03.250
the listening to the birds,

1555
01:05:03.250 --> 01:05:05.210
the smelling of the frangipani,

1556
01:05:05.210 --> 01:05:06.780
the beauty of it all.

1557
01:05:06.780 --> 01:05:08.879
At some point, I actually touched...

1558
01:05:08.879 --> 01:05:12.970
My arm projected longer, so I could touch the ground

1559
01:05:12.970 --> 01:05:14.600
