he was showing up at my office in the early days.

1811
01:16:18.305 --> 01:16:19.138
Yeah.

1812
01:16:19.138 --> 01:16:21.010
Now I'm showing up at city hall room 200

1813
01:16:21.010 --> 01:16:24.800
and being his counseliary .

1814
01:16:24.800 --> 01:16:26.440
I mean, I was sort of like the person

1815
01:16:26.440 --> 01:16:30.070
he sought counsel from, and then he did the same thing.

1816
01:16:30.070 --> 01:16:33.480
So with his like, the chief of police and the fire chief,

1817
01:16:33.480 --> 01:16:35.790
and the head of human services

1818
01:16:35.790 --> 01:16:38.800
and we would actually do offsite retreats (chuckles)

1819
01:16:38.800 --> 01:16:41.020
and I would help mentor some of them.

1820
01:16:41.020 --> 01:16:43.970
And so that was a fascinating experience.

1821
01:16:43.970 --> 01:16:45.740
And then he ultimately became governor

1822
01:16:45.740 --> 01:16:48.400
and we still stay in touch pretty closely.

1823
01:16:48.400 --> 01:16:49.270
It's a trip though.

1824
01:16:49.270 --> 01:16:51.960
Like you're like, what am I doing?

1825
01:16:51.960 --> 01:16:54.000
I mean, mentoring the mayor of San Francisco (laughs).

1826
01:16:54.000 --> 01:16:55.113
Well, it's weird.

1827
01:16:56.540 --> 01:16:57.480
It was like Chip.

1828
01:16:57.480 --> 01:17:00.745
Everybody in the office knew Chip brings Gavin's lunch

1829
01:17:00.745 --> 01:17:03.870
and Chip's lunch in these sacks.

1830
01:17:03.870 --> 01:17:05.180
And there's actually San Francisco magazine

1831
01:17:05.180 --> 01:17:06.013
once did a story about.

1832
01:17:06.013 --> 01:17:08.300
It's like this picture of me coming to Gavin's office

1833
01:17:08.300 --> 01:17:09.693
with a sack lunch.

1834
01:17:12.950 --> 01:17:15.503
And I've been lucky enough to be the mentor

1835
01:17:18.107 --> 01:17:19.300
for the Burning Man, CEO.

1836
01:17:19.300 --> 01:17:23.580
Marian Goodell, Liz Lambert, one of the best known

1837
01:17:23.580 --> 01:17:25.293
boutique hoteliers in the U.S.

1838
01:17:26.380 --> 01:17:28.793
And of course, Brian Chesky and others.

1839
01:17:31.320 --> 01:17:34.430
What I love about mentorship is,

1840
01:17:34.430 --> 01:17:36.760
in a great mentorship relationship

1841
01:17:36.760 --> 01:17:40.270
you as the mentor may learn as much as they will.

1842
01:17:40.270 --> 01:17:43.180
And when I was at Airbnb, I had over 100 mentees

1843
01:17:44.235 --> 01:17:47.840
over the course of seven and a half years, which is a lot.

1844
01:17:47.840 --> 01:17:51.320
And I learned as much from them as they did from me.

1845
01:17:51.320 --> 01:17:53.510
And Brian once said this to me.

1846
01:17:53.510 --> 01:17:54.600
Brian Chesky once said this to me.

1847
01:17:54.600 --> 01:17:58.300
He said, "Chip, you teach me EQ and I teach you DQ."

1848
01:17:58.300 --> 01:18:00.310
EQ being emotional intelligence,

1849
01:18:00.310 --> 01:18:02.540
DQ being digital intelligence.

1850
01:18:02.540 --> 01:18:04.560
And I think if we could look at mentorship,

1851
01:18:04.560 --> 01:18:06.270
we have five generations in the workplace

1852
01:18:06.270 --> 01:18:08.020
for the very first time.

1853
01:18:08.020 --> 01:18:09.730
And if we could realize it.

1854
01:18:09.730 --> 01:18:11.170
And here's a couple other stats,

1855
01:18:11.170 --> 01:18:13.300
for over 40% of Americans now,

1856
01:18:13.300 --> 01:18:15.080
have a boss that's younger than them.

1857
01:18:15.080 --> 01:18:17.670
So my relationship with Brian was not as unusual

1858
01:18:17.670 --> 01:18:20.309
as it might have been 25 years ago.

1859
01:18:20.309 --> 01:18:24.510
So we have something to learn from each other

1860
01:18:24.510 --> 01:18:28.530
and being able to create mutual mentorship relationships,

1861
01:18:28.530 --> 01:18:31.000
where one person knows one thing,

1862
01:18:31.000 --> 01:18:34.340
that the other person wants to learn and vice versa.

1863
01:18:34.340 --> 01:18:37.010
It wouldn't take a lot of matchmaking technology

1864
01:18:37.010 --> 01:18:41.573
in a large company to match employees as mentors.

1865
01:18:41.573 --> 01:18:42.930
Because quite frankly,

1866
01:18:42.930 --> 01:18:45.450
the beautiful thing about a mentorship relationship

1867
01:18:45.450 --> 01:18:50.450
is it's free form, it's affordable, doesn't cost anything.

1868
01:18:53.536 --> 01:18:58.536
And bottom line is Deloitte has shown in their studies

1869
01:18:58.910 --> 01:19:02.600
that when people have a mentor in an organization,

1870
01:19:02.600 --> 01:19:04.380
they are substantially

1871
01:19:04.380 --> 01:19:06.830
more likely to stay in the organization.

1872
01:19:06.830 --> 01:19:08.630
And an era like we are in right now

1873
01:19:08.630 --> 01:19:09.760
with the great resignation,

1874
01:19:09.760 --> 01:19:12.460
where companies are saying we're losing people

1875
01:19:12.460 --> 01:19:14.090
left and right.

1876
01:19:14.090 --> 01:19:15.140
One of the best things you could do

1877
01:19:15.140 --> 01:19:18.620
not just for wisdom and knowledge transfer,

1878
01:19:18.620 --> 01:19:21.530
but also for retention is create mentorship programs

1879
01:19:21.530 --> 01:19:22.540
within an organization.

1880
01:19:22.540 --> 01:19:23.700
Right.

1881
01:19:23.700 --> 01:19:26.660
There's great kinship between the idea of mentorship

1882
01:19:26.660 --> 01:19:28.229
and the modern elder idea, right?

1883
01:19:28.229 --> 01:19:29.580
Yes.

1884
01:19:29.580 --> 01:19:31.730
Before we move off of this, though,

1885
01:19:31.730 --> 01:19:34.410
I think I'd like to hear a few thoughts

1886
01:19:34.410 --> 01:19:37.622
on going about finding a mentor,

1887
01:19:37.622 --> 01:19:41.240
or soliciting your services as a mentor.

1888
01:19:41.240 --> 01:19:43.370
I think there's a lot of people who think mentorship

1889
01:19:43.370 --> 01:19:45.680
means I'm gonna email Seth Godin

1890
01:19:45.680 --> 01:19:47.090
every single day for two years,

1891
01:19:47.090 --> 01:19:49.520
until he finally relents and becomes my mentor.

1892
01:19:49.520 --> 01:19:51.130
We think of these fancy names.

1893
01:19:51.130 --> 01:19:51.963
Yeah.

1894
01:19:51.963 --> 01:19:53.820
People that we would like to have on call,

1895
01:19:53.820 --> 01:19:57.070
but that's not really the heart of what it is.

1896
01:19:57.070 --> 01:19:58.730
Like, I'm always talking about the fact

1897
01:19:58.730 --> 01:20:00.618
that there are mentors all around you.

1898
01:20:00.618 --> 01:20:01.451
That's right.

1899
01:20:01.451 --> 01:20:02.540
Like find the people in your environment,

1900
01:20:02.540 --> 01:20:06.180
and figure out how you can contribute to their lives,

1901
01:20:06.180 --> 01:20:08.830
if you're gonna solicit their input into yours.

1902
01:20:08.830 --> 01:20:09.663
Yeah.

1903
01:20:09.663 --> 01:20:12.175
And be careful of like leading with the M word.

1904
01:20:12.175 --> 01:20:14.690
Like marriage.
Marriage, it's like, whoa.

1905
01:20:14.690 --> 01:20:15.523
The M word, marriage.

1906
01:20:15.523 --> 01:20:19.010
Like if you pop the marriage question on the second day,

1907
01:20:19.010 --> 01:20:19.962
it's absolute mess, right?

1908
01:20:19.962 --> 01:20:20.795
(both laugh)

1909
01:20:20.795 --> 01:20:22.880
I know you.
Yeah, exactly.

1910
01:20:22.880 --> 01:20:25.990
So the way I would go about it on either side

1911
01:20:25.990 --> 01:20:27.120
mentor or mentee.

1912
01:20:27.120 --> 01:20:28.932
But maybe let's start with the mentee side is,

1913
01:20:28.932 --> 01:20:32.170
let's say, there's somebody you wanna learn from.

1914
01:20:32.170 --> 01:20:35.250
Just say, hey, could I take you out for coffee?

1915
01:20:35.250 --> 01:20:36.380
Or could we go have tea?

1916
01:20:36.380 --> 01:20:39.090
Or can we just have a meal together?

1917
01:20:39.090 --> 01:20:42.010
And if they're in your same organization,

1918
01:20:42.010 --> 01:20:44.360
the likelihood is that they're gonna say yes.

1919
01:20:44.360 --> 01:20:47.080
If it's the CEO and you've been in the company

1920
01:20:47.080 --> 01:20:48.860
a month as an intern,

1921
01:20:48.860 --> 01:20:51.550
they may not say yes, if it's a huge company.

1922
01:20:51.550 --> 01:20:55.140
But one of the ways to pose it is to say,

1923
01:20:55.140 --> 01:20:58.120
hey, let's just have a little meal.

1924
01:20:58.120 --> 01:21:00.580
And I just would love to tap into your wisdom.

1925
01:21:00.580 --> 01:21:04.300
I mean, don't be shy about saying something like that.

1926
01:21:04.300 --> 01:21:06.710
Or even more specific you say,

1927
01:21:06.710 --> 01:21:10.400
I've noticed that you're really good at fill in the blank.

1928
01:21:10.400 --> 01:21:12.200
I would like to learn from you.

1929
01:21:12.200 --> 01:21:15.370
Can we just get together once and have a conversation.

1930
01:21:15.370 --> 01:21:18.550
And then don't expect a bigger commitment than that.

1931
01:21:18.550 --> 01:21:21.840
But then have the one time conversation.

1932
01:21:21.840 --> 01:21:23.530
And if it goes really well,

1933
01:21:23.530 --> 01:21:26.530
and you can see that they're enjoying it as the mentor

1934
01:21:27.550 --> 01:21:31.910
say, in a month from now, could we maybe get together again?

1935
01:21:31.910 --> 01:21:34.500
And they're likely to say yes, do it again.

1936
01:21:34.500 --> 01:21:36.900
And at that point, if that second one is great,

1937
01:21:36.900 --> 01:21:40.350
then say, are you open to us maybe creating

1938
01:21:40.350 --> 01:21:43.340
like a short term mentor relationship.

1939
01:21:43.340 --> 01:21:45.720
Maybe we get together once a month,

1940
01:21:45.720 --> 01:21:50.110
or every two weeks, for an hour, and I ask questions

1941
01:21:50.110 --> 01:21:53.668
and you gimme some homework assignments, et cetera.

1942
01:21:53.668 --> 01:21:56.850
At the point where you're ready to ask that,

1943
01:21:56.850 --> 01:21:59.460
get specific about what you're looking for.

1944
01:21:59.460 --> 01:22:02.110
And be clear that you're committed to it.

1945
01:22:02.110 --> 01:22:03.710
I mean, the worst experience for a mentor

1946
01:22:03.710 --> 01:22:06.220
is when a mentee goes through that whole process

1947
01:22:06.220 --> 01:22:07.880
and then doesn't take it seriously.

1948
01:22:07.880 --> 01:22:09.130
Doesn't show up for the meeting.

1949
01:22:09.130 --> 01:22:13.040
Or you know says, okay, well I don't have time right now,

1950
01:22:13.040 --> 01:22:14.140
or et cetera.

1951
01:22:14.140 --> 01:22:15.130
Make the time.

1952
01:22:15.130 --> 01:22:16.290
If you're not willing to make the time,

1953
01:22:16.290 --> 01:22:18.473
then don't actually make the ask.

1954
01:22:20.560 --> 01:22:23.470
But also give it a finite period,

1955
01:22:23.470 --> 01:22:28.050
because that way the other person doesn't feel like, oh, no.

1956
01:22:28.050 --> 01:22:29.311
This is a lifelong commitment.

1957
01:22:29.311 --> 01:22:30.144
Yeah.

1958
01:22:30.144 --> 01:22:31.910
The other thing I would just say,

1959
01:22:31.910 --> 01:22:34.380
is there's two kinds of mentorship.

1960
01:22:34.380 --> 01:22:36.780
There's mentorship, that's performance.

1961
01:22:36.780 --> 01:22:38.210
What I would call performance based,

1962
01:22:38.210 --> 01:22:41.690
and then development based.

1963
01:22:41.690 --> 01:22:43.790
Performance based is like finite.

1964
01:22:43.790 --> 01:22:45.580
It's more of a mentorship relationship

1965
01:22:45.580 --> 01:22:49.560
where you have information I want to learn,

1966
01:22:49.560 --> 01:22:51.680
I need to ask you questions.

1967
01:22:51.680 --> 01:22:53.760
And you'll be my mentor,

1968
01:22:53.760 --> 01:22:54.713
but it'll be a finite relationship

1969
01:22:54.713 --> 01:22:58.735
because I wanna learn more about how to run a great meeting,

1970
01:22:58.735 --> 01:23:02.990
how to understand the travel industry,

1971
01:23:02.990 --> 01:23:07.130
how to I don't know, code.

1972
01:23:07.130 --> 01:23:08.483
To do simple coding.

1973
01:23:10.170 --> 01:23:11.960
So that's finite relationship.

1974
01:23:11.960 --> 01:23:14.700
It's performance-driven in the sense that over the course

1975
01:23:14.700 --> 01:23:15.570
of a short period of time,

1976
01:23:15.570 --> 01:23:18.280
you can determine if you've gotten better at that.

1977
01:23:18.280 --> 01:23:21.050
So you can have a lot of mentees

1978
01:23:21.050 --> 01:23:22.370
if there's sort of like that.

1979
01:23:22.370 --> 01:23:23.500
They're like, okay, yeah,

1980
01:23:23.500 --> 01:23:25.290
you can ask me questions every once in a while.

1981
01:23:25.290 --> 01:23:28.240
And I understand you wanna learn about this thing.

1982
01:23:28.240 --> 01:23:29.250
The other kind of relationship

1983
01:23:29.250 --> 01:23:30.990
is a developmental relationship,

1984
01:23:30.990 --> 01:23:32.790
and it's a lot more comprehensive.

1985
01:23:32.790 --> 01:23:37.790
And it's more personal, professional, emotional, EQ.

1986
01:23:38.830 --> 01:23:41.740
And that's when somebody really just wants a guide.

1987
01:23:41.740 --> 01:23:46.640
You know one of my mentees called me her confidant.

1988
01:23:46.640 --> 01:23:48.160
And I said, wow, okay,

1989
01:23:48.160 --> 01:23:50.070
give me some juicy gossip. (chuckles).

1990
01:23:50.070 --> 01:23:51.770
And she said, well, no, no.

1991
01:23:51.770 --> 01:23:52.603
And she's French.

1992
01:23:52.603 --> 01:23:54.157
She said, "When I say confidant,

1993
01:23:54.157 --> 01:23:55.947
"and I mean it in sort of the French way,

1994
01:23:55.947 --> 01:23:58.420
"you're the one who gives me confidence."

1995
01:23:58.420 --> 01:24:00.940
So it wasn't knowledge transfer.

1996
01:24:00.940 --> 01:24:02.020
It wasn't a knowledge transfer

1997
01:24:02.020 --> 01:24:03.033
which the first kind of relationship is.

1998
01:24:03.033 --> 01:24:04.870
It's like I'm transferring knowledge.

1999
01:24:04.870 --> 01:24:08.680
The second kind of relationship is one that's more personal.

2000
01:24:08.680 --> 01:24:11.280
And actually the person who's asking the questions

2001
01:24:11.280 --> 01:24:12.810
is not the mentee.

2002
01:24:12.810 --> 01:24:16.010
The person who's asking the questions is the mentor.

2003
01:24:16.010 --> 01:24:19.120
And I'm asking questions about you,

2004
01:24:19.120 --> 01:24:21.810
to help you unlock who you are,

2005
01:24:21.810 --> 01:24:26.150
and help you guide you be your permissionery.

2006
01:24:26.150 --> 01:24:28.740
To help give you the permission to do the things

2007
01:24:28.740 --> 01:24:31.310
I think that you have the capability of doing.

2008
01:24:31.310 --> 01:24:32.690
A lot of people think

2009
01:24:32.690 --> 01:24:35.500
that's what a mentorship relationship is, and it is.

2010
01:24:35.500 --> 01:24:38.080
But this first kind is the more finite kind

2011
01:24:38.080 --> 01:24:40.780
and you allows you to have many relationships

2012
01:24:40.780 --> 01:24:43.676
because it's specific to a knowledge transfer.

2013
01:24:43.676 --> 01:24:44.730
Yeah.

2014
01:24:44.730 --> 01:24:47.700
So being able to understand what kind of relationship

2015
01:24:47.700 --> 01:24:50.440
a person wants when it comes to mentorship,

2016
01:24:50.440 --> 01:24:51.850
is pretty important.

2017
01:24:51.850 --> 01:24:53.220
Not to push back,

2018
01:24:53.220 --> 01:24:57.020
but how many emails a day do you get from people

2019
01:24:57.020 --> 01:24:59.352
asking to take you out for a cup of coffee?

2020
01:24:59.352 --> 01:25:00.185
(Chip laughs)

2021
01:25:00.185 --> 01:25:01.340
You see, that's the thing-

2022
01:25:01.340 --> 01:25:03.402
It's nice to live in (indistinct) most of the time,

2023
01:25:03.402 --> 01:25:04.235
because I'm not-

2024
01:25:04.235 --> 01:25:06.023
I Get a lot of those emails and it's just like,

2025
01:25:06.023 --> 01:25:07.370
I can't do that.

2026
01:25:07.370 --> 01:25:09.360
Like, I'm not of a position to do that.

2027
01:25:09.360 --> 01:25:12.080
So I think it is really about who are the people

2028
01:25:12.080 --> 01:25:13.160
in your environment.

2029
01:25:13.160 --> 01:25:13.993
Yeah.

2030
01:25:13.993 --> 01:25:14.860
I mean, when it's...

2031
01:25:14.860 --> 01:25:17.220
Listen, if someone has come from out of the blue

2032
01:25:17.220 --> 01:25:19.370
and I don't know them at all,

2033
01:25:19.370 --> 01:25:21.160
I mean, I do get a lot of emails from people

2034
01:25:21.160 --> 01:25:23.283
and they tweet me up with all the things

2035
01:25:23.283 --> 01:25:25.743
that they know I'll probably want to hear.

2036
01:25:26.950 --> 01:25:29.050
And in those kinds of cases,

2037
01:25:29.050 --> 01:25:31.000
I'm not gonna get on a Zoom call with them,

2038
01:25:31.000 --> 01:25:34.530
but I will answer two or three questions by in an email.

2039
01:25:34.530 --> 01:25:35.550
I'll do that.

2040
01:25:35.550 --> 01:25:37.280
And so for those of you who are listening

2041
01:25:37.280 --> 01:25:38.113
to business now,

2042
01:25:38.113 --> 01:25:39.820
and you wanna send me an email with a couple questions,

2043
01:25:39.820 --> 01:25:40.653
feel free to do that

2044
01:25:40.653 --> 01:25:43.670
because I had people who helped me early on.

2045
01:25:43.670 --> 01:25:45.930
There are times when I may say, oh man,

2046
01:25:45.930 --> 01:25:47.750
I am barrage right now.

2047
01:25:47.750 --> 01:25:49.600
So I'm gonna have my assistant

2048
01:25:49.600 --> 01:25:53.880
actually tell you to write me back in a month or six months.

2049
01:25:53.880 --> 01:25:55.890
And then maybe I can answer some questions.

2050
01:25:55.890 --> 01:25:57.720
But most of the time I do answer questions

2051
01:25:57.720 --> 01:25:58.860
by email pretty quickly,

2052
01:25:58.860 --> 01:26:02.050
assuming they're really simple answers.

2053
01:26:02.050 --> 01:26:03.347
But in terms of like someone saying,

2054
01:26:03.347 --> 01:26:06.220
"Hey, can I get to do a quick Zoom call with you."

2055
01:26:06.220 --> 01:26:08.350
It's like, I think one of the lessons we've learned

2056
01:26:08.350 --> 01:26:09.840
in the last two years,

2057
01:26:09.840 --> 01:26:13.580
is doing a simple Zoom call will wear you out.

2058
01:26:13.580 --> 01:26:16.420
If you do 12 of those in a row.

2059
01:26:16.420 --> 01:26:21.290
And so for me, I also like to test somebody.

2060
01:26:21.290 --> 01:26:23.180
If someone comes to me and says

2061
01:26:23.180 --> 01:26:26.740
they want some of my time and I'm really, really barraged

2062
01:26:26.740 --> 01:26:29.000
with stuff right now, and I can't give any time,

2063
01:26:29.000 --> 01:26:31.210
like come back to me in three months or six months.

2064
01:26:31.210 --> 01:26:33.860
If they come back to me in three or six months,

2065
01:26:33.860 --> 01:26:37.342
and I can see that they've got something,

2066
01:26:37.342 --> 01:26:39.210
I'll take it a little more seriously.

2067
01:26:39.210 --> 01:26:43.380
Not because I didn't take them seriously the first time,

2068
01:26:43.380 --> 01:26:47.053
but because they've shown me they have some reliability,

2069
01:26:49.350 --> 01:26:51.504
and some desire.

2070
01:26:51.504 --> 01:26:55.870
And therefore I'm gonna try to reciprocate if I can.

2071
01:26:55.870 --> 01:26:57.680
I wanna talk about what it means

2072
01:26:57.680 --> 01:27:01.540
to be a modern elder defining what a modern elder is.

2073
01:27:01.540 --> 01:27:03.470
But to kind of contextualize it.

2074
01:27:03.470 --> 01:27:08.070
When I think of your tenure at Airbnb coming in as a mentor,

2075
01:27:08.070 --> 01:27:11.420
kind of emerging from that as this modern elder,

2076
01:27:11.420 --> 01:27:15.540
and the success that relationship engendered

2077
01:27:15.540 --> 01:27:18.410
in terms of the company's prosperity.

2078
01:27:18.410 --> 01:27:22.740
It seems to me that should be like a Harvard Business School

2079
01:27:22.740 --> 01:27:23.573
case study.

2080
01:27:23.573 --> 01:27:25.020
Maybe it is, I don't know.

2081
01:27:25.020 --> 01:27:27.540
But because it was so fruitful and productive,

2082
01:27:27.540 --> 01:27:31.640
why doesn't every company institute some version of that?

2083
01:27:31.640 --> 01:27:34.942
Because it was so valuable.

2084
01:27:34.942 --> 01:27:37.990
The good news is the venture capital community

2085
01:27:37.990 --> 01:27:40.210
and private equity community took notice,

2086
01:27:40.210 --> 01:27:43.770
and it led to a lot of those companies sort of saying,

2087
01:27:43.770 --> 01:27:45.434
how are we going to...

2088
01:27:45.434 --> 01:27:47.910
The way venture capital and private equity works

2089
01:27:47.910 --> 01:27:52.440
is often the person who's put in charge of the young CEO

2090
01:27:52.440 --> 01:27:57.440
is a young partner or young associate at the venture capital

2091
01:27:58.150 --> 01:27:59.370
or private equity firm.

2092
01:27:59.370 --> 01:28:00.703
So they're not necessarily the person who has-

2093
01:28:00.703 --> 01:28:03.600
It's not Bill Burley.
It's not Bill Gurley,

2094
01:28:03.600 --> 01:28:08.600
and it's not somebody who's really gonna have the wisdom

2095
01:28:09.060 --> 01:28:11.460
or maybe the cohonest so to speak,

2096
01:28:11.460 --> 01:28:14.270
to say to the person who's outta control,

2097
01:28:14.270 --> 01:28:16.012
you're outta control.

2098
01:28:16.012 --> 01:28:20.790
So what I think we're starting to see is a recognition

2099
01:28:20.790 --> 01:28:24.160
that for their most high profile successful companies,

2100
01:28:24.160 --> 01:28:28.310
they better have a gray hair in the midst.

2101
01:28:28.310 --> 01:28:31.750
And not just showing up once a month at a meeting,

2102
01:28:31.750 --> 01:28:35.138
but actually creating a mentorship relationship.

2103
01:28:35.138 --> 01:28:37.900
You're also seeing a lot more coaches

2104
01:28:37.900 --> 01:28:41.240
being almost assigned to young CEOs,

2105
01:28:41.240 --> 01:28:43.990
such that the coach has that role.

2106
01:28:43.990 --> 01:28:45.940
Now that's not a bad thing as well.

2107
01:28:45.940 --> 01:28:47.803
Coaches can be spectacular,

2108
01:28:47.803 --> 01:28:50.420
but often if the coach doesn't have

2109
01:28:50.420 --> 01:28:52.061
the operating experience,

2110
01:28:52.061 --> 01:28:55.370
they may not be able to keep up with the young CEO

2111
01:28:55.370 --> 01:28:56.790
when they're talking about what's going on.

2112
01:28:56.790 --> 01:28:57.623
Right.

2113
01:28:57.623 --> 01:28:59.030
And this is pretty specific

2114
01:28:59.030 --> 01:29:03.410
to kind of youth culture driven business startup culture.

2115
01:29:03.410 --> 01:29:04.243
Yep.

2116
01:29:04.243 --> 01:29:06.370
But in truth, like the Fortune 500,

2117
01:29:06.370 --> 01:29:08.640
the Coca-Colas like these huge companies,

2118
01:29:08.640 --> 01:29:10.170
are the opposite.

2119
01:29:10.170 --> 01:29:11.470
It's cool of gray hairs.

2120
01:29:11.470 --> 01:29:14.170
The whole C-suite is ancient.

2121
01:29:14.170 --> 01:29:16.684
It's almost like they need a young mentor

2122
01:29:16.684 --> 01:29:19.080
who will be taken seriously to say,

2123
01:29:19.080 --> 01:29:22.160
here's why you're gonna be completely out of business

2124
01:29:22.160 --> 01:29:23.960
in five years, unless you listen

2125
01:29:23.960 --> 01:29:27.000
to kind of what my generation is looking for.

2126
01:29:27.000 --> 01:29:28.340
Yeah.

2127
01:29:28.340 --> 01:29:29.310
You're exactly right.

2128
01:29:29.310 --> 01:29:30.810
Ageism works in both directions.

2129
01:29:30.810 --> 01:29:33.170
A lot of times when we heard the word ageism,

2130
01:29:33.170 --> 01:29:35.900
at least in my world, I think of like older people

2131
01:29:35.900 --> 01:29:37.710
who are being discriminated against.

2132
01:29:37.710 --> 01:29:39.160
It's the opposite as well,

2133
01:29:39.160 --> 01:29:41.130
young people being discriminated against,

2134
01:29:41.130 --> 01:29:45.100
or not being given the opportunity to have a voice

2135
01:29:46.260 --> 01:29:49.690
in organizations that could use some shaking up.

2136
01:29:49.690 --> 01:29:53.030
So yeah, it definitely goes both ways.

2137
01:29:53.030 --> 01:29:54.651
So, I mean, I think...

2138
01:29:54.651 --> 01:29:58.860
I would say that we're living in an era

2139
01:29:58.860 --> 01:30:03.860
where this idea of an intergenerational potluck

2140
01:30:04.080 --> 01:30:07.120
where we all bring to the table that which we do

2141
01:30:07.120 --> 01:30:09.502
or know best, makes a lot of sense.

2142
01:30:09.502 --> 01:30:10.335
Yeah.

2143
01:30:10.335 --> 01:30:11.830
And there's a huge amount of research on this.

2144
01:30:11.830 --> 01:30:14.570
So diversity often in companies

2145
01:30:14.570 --> 01:30:16.160
doesn't think about age diversity.

2146
01:30:16.160 --> 01:30:19.590
We talk about race, gender, ethnicity, sexual orientation,

2147
01:30:19.590 --> 01:30:22.400
but like, okay, age diversity's important as well.

2148
01:30:22.400 --> 01:30:24.260
And there's a bunch of studies that have come out recently

2149
01:30:24.260 --> 01:30:27.909
that have shown that when you create age diverse teams,

2150
01:30:27.909 --> 01:30:29.690
you get the best of both worlds.

2151
01:30:29.690 --> 01:30:34.267
You get that brilliance, that focus, that quick thinking,

2152
01:30:34.267 --> 01:30:39.267
young spirit, along with the ability to metabolize,

2153
01:30:41.230 --> 01:30:42.940
or think holistically

2154
01:30:44.500 --> 01:30:48.770
and think beyond their own ego,

2155
01:30:48.770 --> 01:30:51.050
and actually create that psychological safety.

2156
01:30:51.050 --> 01:30:53.310
Fascinating study Google did,

2157
01:30:53.310 --> 01:30:56.065
that showed that psychological safety was the number one

2158
01:30:56.065 --> 01:30:58.420
ingredient of effective teams.

2159
01:30:58.420 --> 01:31:00.400
And psychological safety is helped

2160
01:31:00.400 --> 01:31:02.600
by having age diversity on a team.

2161
01:31:02.600 --> 01:31:06.020
So I think we're in the early stages

2162
01:31:06.020 --> 01:31:09.810
of realizing that age is an important diversity metric,

2163
01:31:09.810 --> 01:31:10.690
just like the other ones.

2164
01:31:10.690 --> 01:31:11.530
Sure.

2165
01:31:11.530 --> 01:31:14.290
For that to work, and be productive.

2166
01:31:14.290 --> 01:31:19.160
For that dynamic to be functional between older and younger,

2167
01:31:19.160 --> 01:31:22.500
it does require a level of like respect and humility.

2168
01:31:22.500 --> 01:31:26.080
And the capacity to listen and take seriously

2169
01:31:26.080 --> 01:31:27.320
what the other person is saying.

2170
01:31:27.320 --> 01:31:30.640
Because I think both camps sort of look at each other

2171
01:31:30.640 --> 01:31:31.640
as dumb dumbs.

2172
01:31:31.640 --> 01:31:33.810
Like, oh, these old people, they're checked out,

2173
01:31:33.810 --> 01:31:35.188
they don't know what they're doing.

2174
01:31:35.188 --> 01:31:38.960
These young people their brains

2175
01:31:38.960 --> 01:31:40.020
aren't even fully formed yet,

2176
01:31:40.020 --> 01:31:41.940
why should I listen to these people?

2177
01:31:41.940 --> 01:31:45.120
Yeah, this is part of the reasons there's...

2178
01:31:45.120 --> 01:31:47.430
I'm looking forward to doing something later this year.

2179
01:31:47.430 --> 01:31:48.370
I'll mention it now,

2180
01:31:48.370 --> 01:31:50.500
but we don't even have a website up for it yet.

2181
01:31:50.500 --> 01:31:52.890
There's a guy, Michael Hebb,

2182
01:31:52.890 --> 01:31:54.540
who started "Death Over Dinner."

2183
01:31:54.540 --> 01:31:55.686
So it's a movement globally.

2184
01:31:55.686 --> 01:31:57.610
Over a million people have done this.

2185
01:31:57.610 --> 01:31:59.270
Where they have this Jeffersonian dinners

2186
01:31:59.270 --> 01:32:03.630
topics, conversations about the subject of death.

2187
01:32:03.630 --> 01:32:05.080
And you could have one of three or four

2188
01:32:05.080 --> 01:32:06.130
or five different topics

2189
01:32:06.130 --> 01:32:08.760
that you and a few friends can actually talk about

2190
01:32:08.760 --> 01:32:09.765
over dinner.

2191
01:32:09.765 --> 01:32:12.220
Well, I think generations over dinner.

2192
01:32:12.220 --> 01:32:15.234
Generations over dinner, is an idea whose time has come.

2193
01:32:15.234 --> 01:32:16.067
I like that.

2194
01:32:16.067 --> 01:32:18.110
Which means you bring a bunch of generations.

2195
01:32:18.110 --> 01:32:20.080
Like three generations, five generations,

2196
01:32:20.080 --> 01:32:21.830
maybe even seven generations.

2197
01:32:21.830 --> 01:32:24.079
We have seven generations living still.

2198
01:32:24.079 --> 01:32:25.858
Bring them to a table and have a topic

2199
01:32:25.858 --> 01:32:29.240
about how do you solve climate change?

2200
01:32:29.240 --> 01:32:31.510
How do you address intergenerational collaboration

2201
01:32:31.510 --> 01:32:32.393
in the workplace?

2202
01:32:33.466 --> 01:32:37.109
What happens in the various life stages?

2203
01:32:37.109 --> 01:32:40.450
And the wisdom would go in both directions.

2204
01:32:40.450 --> 01:32:41.283
And you could sort of say,

2205
01:32:41.283 --> 01:32:43.190
oh, we just had a six generation dinner.

2206
01:32:43.190 --> 01:32:44.580
Or we had a four generation dinner.

2207
01:32:44.580 --> 01:32:47.150
And so it's something we're gonna be launching

2208
01:32:47.150 --> 01:32:48.520
later this summer.

2209
01:32:48.520 --> 01:32:50.710
We have a nonprofit called AGE,

2210
01:32:50.710 --> 01:32:52.560
the Association for Growth and Education,

2211
01:32:52.560 --> 01:32:55.236
that focuses on intergenerational collaboration.

2212
01:32:55.236 --> 01:32:57.560
And so keep an eye out for that.

2213
01:32:57.560 --> 01:32:59.000
That's cool, I really like that.

2214
01:32:59.000 --> 01:32:59.860
The "Generations Over Dinner."

2215
01:32:59.860 --> 01:33:02.070
Yeah, I really like that idea.

2216
01:33:02.070 --> 01:33:04.170
All right let's talk about the Modern Elder Academy.

2217
01:33:04.170 --> 01:33:06.980
So walk me through this epiphany,

2218
01:33:06.980 --> 01:33:10.345
and how you've created this amazing thing out of that idea.

2219
01:33:10.345 --> 01:33:11.460
Yeah.

2220
01:33:11.460 --> 01:33:13.180
So I went down to Baja,

2221
01:33:13.180 --> 01:33:15.930
so Southern Baja near Cabo San Lucas,

2222
01:33:15.930 --> 01:33:19.810
about an hour North place called Toes Santos Pescadero area

2223
01:33:19.810 --> 01:33:20.643
on the beach.

2224
01:33:20.643 --> 01:33:21.930
I had a home there.

2225
01:33:21.930 --> 01:33:23.790
And I was gonna start writing my fifth book

2226
01:33:23.790 --> 01:33:26.760
called "Wisdom at Work The Making of a Modern Elder."

2227
01:33:26.760 --> 01:33:27.790
So as I'm writing that book,

2228
01:33:27.790 --> 01:33:29.180
I'm going for runs on the beach.

2229
01:33:29.180 --> 01:33:31.520
And one day I went for a run on the beach

2230
01:33:31.520 --> 01:33:33.660
and I had a Baja aha.

2231
01:33:33.660 --> 01:33:35.560
I had an epiphany.
(laughs)

2232
01:33:35.560 --> 01:33:38.420
As one is, want to do.
Yes, exactly.

2233
01:33:38.420 --> 01:33:40.590
We like, this is an area where there is a midwife

2234
01:33:40.590 --> 01:33:41.423
for epiphanies.

2235
01:33:41.423 --> 01:33:44.820
It's like something strange about this particular area

2236
01:33:44.820 --> 01:33:46.120
that I live in.

2237
01:33:46.120 --> 01:33:49.410
And the epiphany I had was this,

2238
01:33:49.410 --> 01:33:53.700
is that the word adolescence didn't exist in 1903.

2239
01:33:53.700 --> 01:33:58.100
In 1904 it came to into being, because a psychologist

2240
01:33:58.100 --> 01:33:59.637
named Stanley Hall created it and said,

2241
01:33:59.637 --> 01:34:00.877
"Hey, there's a life stage

2242
01:34:00.877 --> 01:34:03.840
"that happens between puberty and 18."

2243
01:34:03.840 --> 01:34:05.460
And you're still a child,

2244
01:34:05.460 --> 01:34:07.780
but you're on the threshold of adulthood.

2245
01:34:07.780 --> 01:34:10.130
And you're going through emotional

2246
01:34:10.130 --> 01:34:14.910
and physical and identity transitions.

2247
01:34:14.910 --> 01:34:15.743
Okay.

2248
01:34:15.743 --> 01:34:19.370
And then after 1904, now we had public junior high schools

2249
01:34:19.370 --> 01:34:21.940
and high schools more prevalent.

2250
01:34:21.940 --> 01:34:23.430
You had child labor laws.

2251
01:34:23.430 --> 01:34:25.500
You had people not getting married at age 14,

2252
01:34:25.500 --> 01:34:27.800
and having babies when they're 15.

2253
01:34:27.800 --> 01:34:31.070
So long story short is adolescence became a thing.

2254
01:34:31.070 --> 01:34:32.290
Well, about 20 years ago,

2255
01:34:32.290 --> 01:34:35.572
a new term got a coin called middlescence.

2256
01:34:35.572 --> 01:34:39.212
Middlescence is the adult corollary to adolescence.

2257
01:34:39.212 --> 01:34:42.050
And it's when you're going through physical and hormonal

2258
01:34:42.050 --> 01:34:44.990
and identity transitions,

2259
01:34:44.990 --> 01:34:48.125
often between around age 40 and 60.

2260
01:34:48.125 --> 01:34:52.000
So the core of midlife.

2261
01:34:52.000 --> 01:34:53.950
So I had this run and I came back and said,

2262
01:34:53.950 --> 01:34:56.470
why do we not have any middlescence school?

2263
01:34:56.470 --> 01:34:59.400
We have adolescent, we give all this guidance

2264
01:34:59.400 --> 01:35:02.500
and schools and tools, and rights of passage and rituals

2265
01:35:02.500 --> 01:35:03.720
for people in adolescence.

2266
01:35:03.720 --> 01:35:05.570
But we have nothing for middlescence.

2267
01:35:06.706 --> 01:35:08.680
And what if we were to create the world's first midlife

2268
01:35:08.680 --> 01:35:09.600
wisdom school?

2269
01:35:09.600 --> 01:35:11.650
A place where people come to cultivate

2270
01:35:11.650 --> 01:35:15.060
and harvest their wisdom, and reimagine and repurpose it.

2271
01:35:15.060 --> 01:35:18.770
And so this was about four and a half years ago

2272
01:35:18.770 --> 01:35:19.603
I had the idea.

2273
01:35:19.603 --> 01:35:21.650
We opened January, 2018,

2274
01:35:21.650 --> 01:35:26.650
and we've had now 2,200 people from 33 countries

2275
01:35:26.863 --> 01:35:28.220
come to our programs.

2276
01:35:28.220 --> 01:35:31.510
Usually a week long program in Baja,

2277
01:35:31.510 --> 01:35:33.500
but we also have programs online as well.

2278
01:35:33.500 --> 01:35:37.660
And the whole intent is to help people

2279
01:35:37.660 --> 01:35:41.390
to reframe their relationship with aging.

2280
01:35:41.390 --> 01:35:44.450
And interestingly enough, the average age is 54,

2281
01:35:44.450 --> 01:35:48.980
but 15%, 14% of the people who come are millennials.

2282
01:35:48.980 --> 01:35:50.210
So this is not just people-

2283
01:35:50.210 --> 01:35:51.140
That's super interesting.

2284
01:35:51.140 --> 01:35:53.477
Isn't interesting, that people in their 30s-

2285
01:35:54.506 --> 01:35:58.070
And the motivation is I just wanna be in a growth mindset

2286
01:35:58.070 --> 01:36:01.077
and thinking about this before it becomes you know-

2287
01:36:01.077 --> 01:36:03.720
And I wanna start to mine my wisdom.

2288
01:36:03.720 --> 01:36:06.550
And I also want to be around people 20 years older than me

2289
01:36:06.550 --> 01:36:09.170
to learn from their wisdom.

2290
01:36:09.170 --> 01:36:11.820
So, yeah, and we have a great faculty.

2291
01:36:11.820 --> 01:36:12.850
We have everybody from Paul Hawken,

2292
01:36:12.850 --> 01:36:16.000
who's been on your show, to Michael Franti, the musician.

2293
01:36:16.000 --> 01:36:18.473
Matthieu Ricard, the famous Buddhist monk,

2294
01:36:19.430 --> 01:36:22.160
Sherry Lansing has done shows for us.

2295
01:36:22.160 --> 01:36:24.560
John Donahoe, CEO of Nike

2296
01:36:24.560 --> 01:36:28.210
is on our upcoming purpose online course.

2297
01:36:28.210 --> 01:36:30.944
So lots of really interesting people

2298
01:36:30.944 --> 01:36:35.944
who are helping all of us

2299
01:36:36.350 --> 01:36:39.640
to reframe that relationship with aging

2300
01:36:39.640 --> 01:36:41.840
and look at how we might regenerate ourselves.

2301
01:36:41.840 --> 01:36:46.240
Because the narrative we have about aging is you age,

2302
01:36:46.240 --> 01:36:47.700
you work and you're retired.

2303
01:36:47.700 --> 01:36:49.250
You have three stages in life.

2304
01:36:49.250 --> 01:36:51.610
You learn till you're 20 or 25.

2305
01:36:51.610 --> 01:36:54.050
You earn 'til you're 60 or 65.

2306
01:36:54.050 --> 01:36:56.400
And then you retire till you die.

2307
01:36:56.400 --> 01:36:57.930
And if you look at a millennial today, they're like,

2308
01:36:57.930 --> 01:37:00.810
what the hell is that tyranny of the three stage life?

2309
01:37:00.810 --> 01:37:02.790
I wanna actually work.

2310
01:37:02.790 --> 01:37:04.087
I'm gonna learn until I'm 20 or 25,

2311
01:37:04.087 --> 01:37:06.340
and then I'm gonna work for 10 years

2312
01:37:06.340 --> 01:37:07.400
and then I'm gonna take a year off.

2313
01:37:07.400 --> 01:37:08.750
I'm gonna do a gap here.

2314
01:37:08.750 --> 01:37:11.740
And then I'm gonna go get a master's, I'm gonna learn again.

2315
01:37:11.740 --> 01:37:13.020
And then I'm gonna go retire.

2316
01:37:13.020 --> 01:37:14.820
I'm gonna work for 10 years and start a company

2317
01:37:14.820 --> 01:37:18.400
and then retire, and then go back and get a PhD.

2318
01:37:18.400 --> 01:37:20.710
And it's more episodic.
Yeah.

2319
01:37:20.710 --> 01:37:23.303
It's not like you have these three stages of life

2320
01:37:23.303 --> 01:37:24.630
that are age defined.

2321
01:37:24.630 --> 01:37:29.630
And so that led us with MEA becoming very popular

2322
01:37:31.440 --> 01:37:35.170
and led us to creating regenerative communities.

2323
01:37:35.170 --> 01:37:37.440
And these idea of, let's get rid of the idea

2324
01:37:37.440 --> 01:37:38.533
of retirement communities.

2325
01:37:38.533 --> 01:37:40.060
Yeah, this is the real exciting part.

2326
01:37:40.060 --> 01:37:42.204
Rich are you gonna live in retirement communities someday?

2327
01:37:42.204 --> 01:37:46.990
It's the most depressing, horrific aspect of our culture

2328
01:37:46.990 --> 01:37:50.020
to just warehouse old people and hide them.

2329
01:37:50.020 --> 01:37:51.560
It's a form of age apartheid.

2330
01:37:51.560 --> 01:37:54.440
Let's like take those people and put them in a place

2331
01:37:54.440 --> 01:37:57.120
where we can't see them, so they're with each other.

2332
01:37:57.120 --> 01:37:59.394
And let's also recognize that frankly,

2333
01:37:59.394 --> 01:38:02.600
earlier generations sort of saw it

2334
01:38:02.600 --> 01:38:04.830
and our parents may have enjoyed it.

2335
01:38:04.830 --> 01:38:09.460
But there's also this weird sense that back in the old days,

2336
01:38:09.460 --> 01:38:12.070
if you were working on the factory floor,

2337
01:38:12.070 --> 01:38:17.070
or you were a school teacher your whole life,

2338
01:38:18.410 --> 01:38:19.940
you got to 60 and you said,

2339
01:38:19.940 --> 01:38:22.200
thank God I'm retiring,

2340
01:38:22.200 --> 01:38:25.140
because I can't do the factory floor work anymore.

2341
01:38:25.140 --> 01:38:28.610
Or I'm just so tired of this work,

2342
01:38:28.610 --> 01:38:31.850
or being a house cleaner or whatever it was.

2343
01:38:31.850 --> 01:38:34.930
And we have a lot more knowledge workers today.

2344
01:38:34.930 --> 01:38:37.020
And I think we have a lot more wisdom workers.

2345
01:38:37.020 --> 01:38:39.740
That's a phrase that I think is gonna come into being.

2346
01:38:39.740 --> 01:38:42.490
But the knowledge workers they can work till their 70s

2347
01:38:42.490 --> 01:38:44.490
or their 80s, or whenever.

2348
01:38:44.490 --> 01:38:46.980
It's not physical labor that actually defines

2349
01:38:46.980 --> 01:38:48.490
how they get paid.

2350
01:38:48.490 --> 01:38:51.040
And so more and more of them are of the mindset.

2351
01:38:51.040 --> 01:38:53.600
Like I don't wanna retire,

2352
01:38:53.600 --> 01:38:55.020
or I can't afford to retire.

2353
01:38:55.020 --> 01:38:58.940
If I'm gonna live 'til 90 or 95, I can't retire at 62.

2354
01:38:58.940 --> 01:39:03.130
And also that generation is far more inclined

2355
01:39:03.130 --> 01:39:06.232
to be pursuing work that has personal meaning to them.

2356
01:39:06.232 --> 01:39:10.400
We're our legacy is that three chapter

2357
01:39:10.400 --> 01:39:11.990
kind of approach to life.

2358
01:39:11.990 --> 01:39:13.200
That's what our parents did.

2359
01:39:13.200 --> 01:39:15.910
And we kind of were reared with that sensibility.

2360
01:39:15.910 --> 01:39:17.802
And now we're in our 50s and 60s,

2361
01:39:17.802 --> 01:39:22.480
and a lot of us are having those kind of crisis moments.

2362
01:39:22.480 --> 01:39:25.550
But over the course of those many decades, leading up to it,

2363
01:39:25.550 --> 01:39:28.490
there is a calcification around mindset.

2364
01:39:28.490 --> 01:39:29.360
This is who I am.

2365
01:39:29.360 --> 01:39:30.730
This is what I do.

2366
01:39:30.730 --> 01:39:32.440
Here's what I can expect for my life.

2367
01:39:32.440 --> 01:39:33.800
Here's how I see the world.

2368
01:39:33.800 --> 01:39:35.800
This is the people that I vote for.

2369
01:39:35.800 --> 01:39:39.080
And these are the things that I talk about.

2370
01:39:39.080 --> 01:39:43.650
So one of the critical kind of tools that you leverage

2371
01:39:43.650 --> 01:39:45.050
at the Modern Elder Academy,

2372
01:39:45.050 --> 01:39:46.530
that I wanna hear a little bit more about

2373
01:39:46.530 --> 01:39:50.060
is just getting really clear on what your mindset is.

2374
01:39:50.060 --> 01:39:52.870
So that you can begin the process of deconstructing it

2375
01:39:52.870 --> 01:39:55.270
and perhaps telling yourself a new story.

2376
01:39:55.270 --> 01:39:56.103
Yeah.

2377
01:39:56.103 --> 01:39:58.980
Well, so there's four key pillars of our curriculum.

2378
01:39:58.980 --> 01:40:01.020
The first one's reframing aging,

2379
01:40:01.020 --> 01:40:03.620
helping people to see that maybe their best years

2380
01:40:03.620 --> 01:40:04.500
are ahead of them.

2381
01:40:04.500 --> 01:40:05.920
Can this be aspirational?

2382
01:40:05.920 --> 01:40:06.753
It can be.

2383
01:40:06.753 --> 01:40:08.452
And some things get better with age.

2384
01:40:08.452 --> 01:40:12.030
I love that your listeners and your community

2385
01:40:12.030 --> 01:40:14.763
is very much about keeping the body alive.

2386
01:40:16.690 --> 01:40:18.650
Certain things get actually better with age.

2387
01:40:18.650 --> 01:40:19.790
You know your emotion.

2388
01:40:19.790 --> 01:40:22.150
Your emotional intelligence grows with age,

2389
01:40:22.150 --> 01:40:25.690
your spiritual awareness grows with age.

2390
01:40:25.690 --> 01:40:26.523
So there's a lot of things

2391
01:40:26.523 --> 01:40:27.890
that actually get better with age.

2392
01:40:27.890 --> 01:40:29.760
And yes, if you work on your body

2393
01:40:29.760 --> 01:40:31.520
and your health, and your nutrition,

2394
01:40:31.520 --> 01:40:33.820
it might get better with age as well.

2395
01:40:33.820 --> 01:40:35.650
You're a great example of that.

2396
01:40:35.650 --> 01:40:38.470
So there's that reframing aging.

2397
01:40:38.470 --> 01:40:40.000
Then there's mindset.

2398
01:40:40.000 --> 01:40:42.150
So there's growth in fixed mindset.

2399
01:40:42.150 --> 01:40:44.140
We're big fans of Carol Dweck's work at Stanford.

2400
01:40:44.140 --> 01:40:47.900
And so a fixed mindset is when you tend to think of life,

2401
01:40:47.900 --> 01:40:50.960
as I'm here to prove myself,

2402
01:40:50.960 --> 01:40:53.170
and I define success as winning.

2403
01:40:53.170 --> 01:40:55.750
The problem if you have that point of view,

2404
01:40:55.750 --> 01:40:59.240
as you get older is you stop playing

2405
01:40:59.240 --> 01:41:01.180
the games you can't win.

2406
01:41:01.180 --> 01:41:04.600
And that means your sandbox gets smaller and smaller.

2407
01:41:04.600 --> 01:41:07.730
And as it gets smaller, you actually get more bored

2408
01:41:07.730 --> 01:41:09.860
because you're not trying new things.

2409
01:41:09.860 --> 01:41:11.850
So moving from a fix to a growth mindset,

2410
01:41:11.850 --> 01:41:15.930
means moving from proving yourself, to improving yourself.

2411
01:41:15.930 --> 01:41:20.930
And instead of focusing on winning, you focus on learning.

2412
01:41:21.650 --> 01:41:23.780
And so you move into this way of thinking

2413
01:41:23.780 --> 01:41:27.700
and way of being of, okay, I am just a learning machine.

2414
01:41:27.700 --> 01:41:30.130
I'm not a machine, I'm a learning human.

2415
01:41:30.130 --> 01:41:32.370
One of the questions we ask at MEA is

2416
01:41:33.450 --> 01:41:34.570
what is it that you know now,

2417
01:41:34.570 --> 01:41:37.700
or have you done now, that you wish you had known

2418
01:41:37.700 --> 01:41:39.510
or done 10 years ago?

2419
01:41:39.510 --> 01:41:41.010
Think about that for a moment.

2420
01:41:42.060 --> 01:41:44.900
And then ask yourself 10 years from now,

2421
01:41:44.900 --> 01:41:48.900
what will you regret if you don't learn it or do it now?

2422
01:41:48.900 --> 01:41:52.053
And this is how I started to learn how to surf at age 57,

2423
01:41:53.360 --> 01:41:56.440
because I live on a beach near a surf break,

2424
01:41:56.440 --> 01:41:58.010
somewhat famous one.

2425
01:41:58.010 --> 01:42:00.450
And I was like, ain't gonna be any easier

2426
01:42:00.450 --> 01:42:02.750
at 67 than 57, Chip.

2427
01:42:02.750 --> 01:42:03.910
So I gotta start learning that.

2428
01:42:03.910 --> 01:42:06.290
I also started learning Spanish.

2429
01:42:06.290 --> 01:42:08.050
Again, the mantra in my head,

2430
01:42:08.050 --> 01:42:10.730
the mindset was I'm too old for this,

2431
01:42:10.730 --> 01:42:11.890
too old to learn the language.

2432
01:42:11.890 --> 01:42:14.450
But harder to learn it at 67 than 57.

2433
01:42:14.450 --> 01:42:16.830
And I knew that I was gonna be living in Mexico,

2434
01:42:16.830 --> 01:42:18.260
so why not learn it?

2435
01:42:18.260 --> 01:42:20.210
So the first is reframing aging.

2436
01:42:20.210 --> 01:42:21.830
The second is shifting your mindset.

2437
01:42:21.830 --> 01:42:24.750
The third is learning about transitions.

2438
01:42:24.750 --> 01:42:28.230
None of us were taught and got a master's in TQ,

2439
01:42:28.230 --> 01:42:30.040
transitional intelligence.

2440
01:42:30.040 --> 01:42:31.840
But midlife is full of transitions.

2441
01:42:31.840 --> 01:42:33.160
Life is full of transitions.

2442
01:42:33.160 --> 01:42:36.760
Life is liminal you're usually in between two things.

2443
01:42:36.760 --> 01:42:38.660
And yet we've never really been taught.

2444
01:42:38.660 --> 01:42:41.250
What's the anatomy of that transition,

2445
01:42:41.250 --> 01:42:44.670
and how do I architect moving through a transition,

2446
01:42:44.670 --> 01:42:46.870
whether it's getting divorced,

2447
01:42:46.870 --> 01:42:51.870
or changing where I live, or changing my career or retiring,

2448
01:42:54.000 --> 01:42:57.710
or seeing my parents to passing away.

2449
01:42:57.710 --> 01:43:00.540
Or becoming an empty nester, or going through menopause,

2450
01:43:00.540 --> 01:43:02.700
or men go through andropause.

2451
01:43:02.700 --> 01:43:04.820
There's a lot of transitions in midlife,

2452
01:43:04.820 --> 01:43:07.870
and learning how to understand the three stages

2453
01:43:07.870 --> 01:43:09.850
of a transition is a big part of our program.

2454
01:43:09.850 --> 01:43:13.320
And then the final piece of the curriculum is regeneration.

2455
01:43:13.320 --> 01:43:14.360
In all its forms.

2456
01:43:14.360 --> 01:43:15.780
We have regenerative cell therapy.

2457
01:43:15.780 --> 01:43:17.310
I mean, stem cell work.

2458
01:43:17.310 --> 01:43:20.780
Regeneration through understanding a regenerative purpose.

2459
01:43:20.780 --> 01:43:22.980
But also, and this is where Paul Hawken comes in

2460
01:43:22.980 --> 01:43:25.210
regenerative agriculture and farming.

2461
01:43:25.210 --> 01:43:28.000
And that's what leads us to our regenerative communities.

2462
01:43:28.000 --> 01:43:30.820
Our first one being in Baja with 26 homes

2463
01:43:30.820 --> 01:43:32.530
around a regenerative farm.

2464
01:43:32.530 --> 01:43:34.440
So instead of living on a fairway,

2465
01:43:34.440 --> 01:43:36.370
wouldn't you like to live on a farm.

2466
01:43:36.370 --> 01:43:41.070
And go out into the farm and go harvest with your neighbors

2467
01:43:41.070 --> 01:43:43.276
and do a potluck once a week.

2468
01:43:43.276 --> 01:43:45.380
And so now we're taking this to Santa Fe.

2469
01:43:45.380 --> 01:43:47.980
And we have three different properties in Santa Fe,

2470
01:43:47.980 --> 01:43:49.320
two of which will be academies,

2471
01:43:49.320 --> 01:43:52.362
one of which will be a regenerative residential community.

2472
01:43:52.362 --> 01:43:56.900
And I do believe that we are creating something

2473
01:43:56.900 --> 01:43:59.681
that is meant to disrupt senior living.

2474
01:43:59.681 --> 01:44:00.514
Right.

2475
01:44:00.514 --> 01:44:05.514
It disrupts higher education, some form of education.

2476
01:44:06.480 --> 01:44:11.180
And then this whole kind of area that's so bright

2477
01:44:11.180 --> 01:44:12.730
for disruption.

2478
01:44:12.730 --> 01:44:14.230
Yeah.
Senior living.

2479
01:44:14.230 --> 01:44:16.620
But it's leveraging all this experience

2480
01:44:16.620 --> 01:44:18.640
that you had as a hotelier.

2481
01:44:18.640 --> 01:44:20.560
Like it's not that dissimilar.

2482
01:44:20.560 --> 01:44:23.176
Like, oh, I'm gonna disrupt the hotel industry.

2483
01:44:23.176 --> 01:44:25.600
I understand that skillset.

2484
01:44:25.600 --> 01:44:27.770
Now I'm gonna apply it to this other area

2485
01:44:27.770 --> 01:44:31.772
that desperately we need something better and healthier.

2486
01:44:31.772 --> 01:44:32.605
I think that's really exciting.

2487
01:44:32.605 --> 01:44:33.438
I've been lucky.

2488
01:44:33.438 --> 01:44:35.290
Lucky to a disruptor twice in the hospitality industry.

2489
01:44:35.290 --> 01:44:39.307
First as a boutique hotelier, and then at Airbnb.

2490
01:44:39.307 --> 01:44:41.390
You know, you don't start something to disrupt it.

2491
01:44:41.390 --> 01:44:43.090
I mean, that's not the intent.

2492
01:44:43.090 --> 01:44:44.719
You start something because you have an idea.

2493
01:44:44.719 --> 01:44:46.040
It's a natural outgrowth of everything you're doing.

2494
01:44:46.040 --> 01:44:46.873
That's right.

2495
01:44:46.873 --> 01:44:49.578
You have an idea, and then the idea sort of takes off.

2496
01:44:49.578 --> 01:44:52.580
And then we hear from people saying,

2497
01:44:52.580 --> 01:44:54.257
Chip, long life learning.

2498
01:44:54.257 --> 01:44:55.300
That's what you're doing.

2499
01:44:55.300 --> 01:44:56.240
You're not doing lifelong learning.

2500
01:44:56.240 --> 01:44:57.810
You're doing long life learning.

2501
01:44:57.810 --> 01:45:00.870
And more and more colleges and universities

2502
01:45:00.870 --> 01:45:02.560
who are in the process of being disrupted

2503
01:45:02.560 --> 01:45:04.190
in all kinds of ways right now.

2504
01:45:04.190 --> 01:45:06.760
Clay Christiansen, the guy who created the term

2505
01:45:06.760 --> 01:45:08.270
disruptive innovation.

2506
01:45:08.270 --> 01:45:09.650
Before he passed away, five years ago,

2507
01:45:09.650 --> 01:45:13.880
said 50% of the colleges and universities

2508
01:45:13.880 --> 01:45:15.390
in the United States will be out of business

2509
01:45:15.390 --> 01:45:17.030
in 10 to 15 years.

2510
01:45:17.030 --> 01:45:19.800
So colleges and universities need to start thinking

2511
01:45:19.800 --> 01:45:22.750
about midlife wisdom schools, or these places-

2512
01:45:22.750 --> 01:45:23.583
And to be fair,

2513
01:45:23.583 --> 01:45:25.280
a lot of colleges are doing programs,

2514
01:45:25.280 --> 01:45:28.210
but it's sort of traditional curriculum oriented.

2515
01:45:28.210 --> 01:45:29.670
It's accumulating knowledge.

2516
01:45:29.670 --> 01:45:30.987
It's more like I'm going-

2517
01:45:30.987 --> 01:45:32.141
It's not experiential.

2518
01:45:32.141 --> 01:45:35.620
I'm a 50 year old sitting in a classroom of 20 year olds

2519
01:45:35.620 --> 01:45:40.230
and I'm learning biology or I'm learning philosophy.

2520
01:45:40.230 --> 01:45:41.405
But it actually isn't...

2521
01:45:41.405 --> 01:45:43.590
I wrote a white paper

2522
01:45:43.590 --> 01:45:46.490
called the emergence of long life learning.

2523
01:45:46.490 --> 01:45:49.670
To study with a PhD who wrote it with me,

2524
01:45:49.670 --> 01:45:52.690
how do people learn differently at 55?

2525
01:45:52.690 --> 01:45:55.450
What's important to them, how do they learn differently?

2526
01:45:55.450 --> 01:45:57.110
And that actually had a huge impact

2527
01:45:57.110 --> 01:45:59.260
on how we have created our curriculum,

2528
01:45:59.260 --> 01:46:01.166
because we actually at MEA,

2529
01:46:01.166 --> 01:46:04.370
wisdom is not taught it's shared.

2530
01:46:04.370 --> 01:46:05.720
So how do you create the crucible

2531
01:46:05.720 --> 01:46:09.710
for a collection of 20 people over the course of a week?

2532
01:46:09.710 --> 01:46:12.150
To learn a bunch of things,

2533
01:46:12.150 --> 01:46:13.740
but then actually share experience

2534
01:46:13.740 --> 01:46:17.270
and in small diad partners, one on one with each other,

2535
01:46:17.270 --> 01:46:20.410
have these conversations of how it's applicable to them.

2536
01:46:20.410 --> 01:46:24.330
And how it's gonna be applicable to you at 55 or at 45

2537
01:46:24.330 --> 01:46:26.350
is different than it is at 25.

2538
01:46:26.350 --> 01:46:30.030
So yes, lifelong learning is a thing.

2539
01:46:30.030 --> 01:46:33.560
But long life learning is a subset of it,

2540
01:46:33.560 --> 01:46:36.240
focused on how to live a life that's as deep and meaningful

2541
01:46:36.240 --> 01:46:37.370
as it is long.

2542
01:46:37.370 --> 01:46:40.190
Because we have gotten the quantity right.

2543
01:46:40.190 --> 01:46:42.570
Longevity has grown a lot.

2544
01:46:42.570 --> 01:46:44.170
The quality is the thing

2545
01:46:44.170 --> 01:46:46.270
that I think people need to focus more on.

2546
01:46:47.209 --> 01:46:51.900
A big piece in trying to engender your life

2547
01:46:51.900 --> 01:46:53.420
with that level of quality

2548
01:46:53.420 --> 01:46:56.090
is this transition that you speak about

2549
01:46:56.090 --> 01:46:58.950
from accumulation to attunement?

2550
01:46:58.950 --> 01:47:00.940
So speak about that a little bit.

2551
01:47:00.940 --> 01:47:03.500
Yeah, the first half for our life is about accumulating.

2552
01:47:03.500 --> 01:47:05.840
Accumulating knowledge and friends

2553
01:47:05.840 --> 01:47:08.730
and obligations and romantic partners,

2554
01:47:08.730 --> 01:47:11.690
and then marriage possibly and children.

2555
01:47:11.690 --> 01:47:15.270
You get to age 45 or so, and you say, oh my god,

2556
01:47:15.270 --> 01:47:16.410
I've got this burden.

2557
01:47:16.410 --> 01:47:18.470
I've got a lot of baggage.

2558
01:47:18.470 --> 01:47:19.680
And the second half of your life

2559
01:47:19.680 --> 01:47:22.080
is now about editing that baggage.

2560
01:47:22.080 --> 01:47:24.470
So you move from accumulating to editing,

2561
01:47:24.470 --> 01:47:26.960
and you move from the attainment part of your life.

2562
01:47:26.960 --> 01:47:28.420
And it doesn't mean you don't have attainment

2563
01:47:28.420 --> 01:47:30.200
throughout your life, you do.

2564
01:47:30.200 --> 01:47:31.600
But I gotta tell you,

2565
01:47:31.600 --> 01:47:34.513
and as someone who's like a first class attainer,

2566
01:47:35.550 --> 01:47:39.410
your attainment at some point as your ego starts to shift

2567
01:47:39.410 --> 01:47:42.030
that operating system of the ego shifts to the soul,

2568
01:47:42.030 --> 01:47:45.350
you move from attaining to attuning.

2569
01:47:45.350 --> 01:47:46.450
What's the difference?

2570
01:47:47.440 --> 01:47:48.800
When you're in the attainment mode,

2571
01:47:48.800 --> 01:47:51.138
you will atone later because you may have sharp elbows.

2572
01:47:51.138 --> 01:47:52.140
(Rich laughs)

2573
01:47:52.140 --> 01:47:53.286
You're sort of like,

2574
01:47:53.286 --> 01:47:55.640
when you're actually attaining,

2575
01:47:55.640 --> 01:47:57.040
you're very selfishly focused.

2576
01:47:57.040 --> 01:48:00.350
And you might be a gracious person,

2577
01:48:00.350 --> 01:48:05.000
but you are deeply focused on the finish line.

2578
01:48:06.019 --> 01:48:07.900
And that's the hedonic treadmill.

2579
01:48:07.900 --> 01:48:09.060
It's when we sort of realized,

2580
01:48:09.060 --> 01:48:11.240
man, I keep putting finish lines there

2581
01:48:12.120 --> 01:48:14.300
and I feel a sense of accomplishment,

2582
01:48:14.300 --> 01:48:17.600
