Well, I think that's a good place

2840
01:59:42.020 --> 01:59:43.850
to kind of lead into the last thing,

2841
01:59:43.850 --> 01:59:45.050
that I want to talk to you about.

2842
01:59:45.050 --> 01:59:48.650
Which is for that person who is contemplating

2843
01:59:48.650 --> 01:59:49.580
all of this right now,

2844
01:59:49.580 --> 01:59:51.860
perhaps they're in their 40s or their 50s.

2845
01:59:51.860 --> 01:59:53.460
And they're thinking about what's next,

2846
01:59:53.460 --> 01:59:56.620
or if there is even going to be anything next.

2847
01:59:56.620 --> 01:59:59.167
And maybe doesn't have that level

2848
01:59:59.167 --> 02:00:02.170
of self understanding or self integration

2849
02:00:02.170 --> 02:00:04.773
to really have the intuition

2850
02:00:04.773 --> 02:00:09.773
to follow wherever they're being led by their curiosity.

2851
02:00:10.004 --> 02:00:12.320
And perhaps they don't have the budget

2852
02:00:12.320 --> 02:00:16.520
to go down to Baja and participate in your academy.

2853
02:00:16.520 --> 02:00:19.200
What are some things that they could be thinking about,

2854
02:00:19.200 --> 02:00:20.910
perhaps books they could be reading

2855
02:00:20.910 --> 02:00:25.760
or ways of approaching that level of self inquiry

2856
02:00:25.760 --> 02:00:28.670
that could help unlock some of those answers.

2857
02:00:28.670 --> 02:00:31.077
Lots of great books out there.

2858
02:00:31.077 --> 02:00:31.910
"Wisdom at Work."

2859
02:00:31.910 --> 02:00:32.980
"Wisdom at Work," being one of them.

2860
02:00:32.980 --> 02:00:36.630
But "The 100-Year Life" is a really interesting book

2861
02:00:36.630 --> 02:00:41.630
written by two UK academics talking about the medical world

2862
02:00:45.360 --> 02:00:47.560
says that children being born today,

2863
02:00:47.560 --> 02:00:50.390
there's a 50% chance they're gonna live to 100.

2864
02:00:50.390 --> 02:00:54.070
So how would you curate your life differently

2865
02:00:54.070 --> 02:00:55.840
if you knew you were gonna live 'til 100?

2866
02:00:55.840 --> 02:00:56.903
So it's a great book.

2867
02:00:57.930 --> 02:01:01.933
There's gosh, what other books do I love?

2868
02:01:02.960 --> 02:01:04.127
Let me just...

2869
02:01:04.127 --> 02:01:06.530
When you mention that something comes to mind,

2870
02:01:06.530 --> 02:01:08.230
which is another tension.

2871
02:01:08.230 --> 02:01:13.070
The tension between yes, let's contemplate our lives at 100

2872
02:01:13.070 --> 02:01:15.260
and how we can plan for that.

2873
02:01:15.260 --> 02:01:17.550
But how do you like sort of reconcile that

2874
02:01:17.550 --> 02:01:19.680
with another powerful idea,

2875
02:01:19.680 --> 02:01:21.440
which is what if you don't have?

2876
02:01:21.440 --> 02:01:23.680
What if you only have three months or six months

2877
02:01:23.680 --> 02:01:24.513
or a year to live?

2878
02:01:24.513 --> 02:01:27.140
How would that change how you're approaching

2879
02:01:27.140 --> 02:01:28.430
the way that you spend your time?

2880
02:01:28.430 --> 02:01:29.610
Well, that's a very different question.

2881
02:01:29.610 --> 02:01:32.180
So I'm gonna come back to that one.

2882
02:01:32.180 --> 02:01:33.810
Well, I'll go to that one now.

2883
02:01:33.810 --> 02:01:37.350
So Laura Carstensen, I'm on her advisory board

2884
02:01:37.350 --> 02:01:39.410
at the Stanford Center on longevity,

2885
02:01:39.410 --> 02:01:41.730
and she's done very conclusive research

2886
02:01:41.730 --> 02:01:45.180
on this to show that the shorter period of time you have

2887
02:01:45.180 --> 02:01:46.013
in your life,

2888
02:01:46.013 --> 02:01:49.530
the more you take seriously each moment.

2889
02:01:49.530 --> 02:01:51.240
It makes sense.

2890
02:01:51.240 --> 02:01:52.990
And she looked at it with older people,

2891
02:01:52.990 --> 02:01:54.825
but also people who had aids,

2892
02:01:54.825 --> 02:01:59.825
people who had a cancer diagnosis that was incurable.

2893
02:02:00.740 --> 02:02:05.330
And what they found was that when you have less time,

2894
02:02:05.330 --> 02:02:08.020
you are more mindful in the moment.

2895
02:02:08.020 --> 02:02:09.050
And that makes sense.

2896
02:02:09.050 --> 02:02:10.840
You're less focused on the past.

2897
02:02:10.840 --> 02:02:12.970
You're less focused on the future.

2898
02:02:12.970 --> 02:02:14.770
You're more focused on the moment.

2899
02:02:14.770 --> 02:02:15.603
And what comes from that

2900
02:02:15.603 --> 02:02:20.110
is a certain level of life satisfaction and contentment.

2901
02:02:20.110 --> 02:02:23.010
And so that is...

2902
02:02:23.010 --> 02:02:25.470
I would just say for anybody who's looking at their life

2903
02:02:25.470 --> 02:02:27.317
right now and saying,

2904
02:02:27.317 --> 02:02:29.470
"I don't feel very content."

2905
02:02:29.470 --> 02:02:31.070
My response to them would be,

2906
02:02:31.070 --> 02:02:33.770
how much are you spending time in the moment?

2907
02:02:33.770 --> 02:02:34.603
How can you do that?

2908
02:02:34.603 --> 02:02:35.436
Yes, you can meditate.

2909
02:02:35.436 --> 02:02:36.550
Yes you can do yoga.

2910
02:02:36.550 --> 02:02:39.540
Go for a walk with your dog in nature.

2911
02:02:39.540 --> 02:02:40.540
So during the pandemic,

2912
02:02:40.540 --> 02:02:42.380
when I was living in Baja and we had no guests

2913
02:02:42.380 --> 02:02:44.335
because we had to shut down.

2914
02:02:44.335 --> 02:02:45.520
I would do...

2915
02:02:45.520 --> 02:02:49.460
I'd put on my calendar three hours a day, three days a week.

2916
02:02:49.460 --> 02:02:51.650
And I called it spying on the divine.

2917
02:02:51.650 --> 02:02:55.000
And I would go get divinely intoxicated in nature.

2918
02:02:55.000 --> 02:02:57.820
And I don't mean I brought alcohol or psychedelics, no.

2919
02:02:57.820 --> 02:03:01.140
I was going for a walk with my dog for three hours

2920
02:03:01.140 --> 02:03:04.960
in different, natural, beautiful places around Baja.

2921
02:03:04.960 --> 02:03:08.050
And just noticing what nature could teach me.

2922
02:03:08.050 --> 02:03:11.683
The resilience of nature, the interdependence of nature.

2923
02:03:12.560 --> 02:03:13.860
I was being in the moment.

2924
02:03:15.273 --> 02:03:16.160
And our friend Dr. Ketner,

2925
02:03:16.160 --> 02:03:18.630
who started the greater good science center at UC Berkeley

2926
02:03:18.630 --> 02:03:23.630
and is on our faculty and has a book coming out next year.

2927
02:03:23.910 --> 02:03:28.010
Talks about the various forms of how you can find awe

2928
02:03:28.010 --> 02:03:29.080
in your life.

2929
02:03:29.080 --> 02:03:31.700
And for so many people it's found in nature.

2930
02:03:31.700 --> 02:03:33.780
So I'd say that is something you can do.

2931
02:03:33.780 --> 02:03:35.255
You've gotta park near you.

2932
02:03:35.255 --> 02:03:37.600
You don't have to spend any money on that.

2933
02:03:37.600 --> 02:03:40.371
You don't have to buy a book for that.

2934
02:03:40.371 --> 02:03:42.220
But there's lots of other good books.

2935
02:03:42.220 --> 02:03:44.530
There's "From Strength To Strength" that just came out

2936
02:03:44.530 --> 02:03:45.370
with Arthur Brook.

2937
02:03:45.370 --> 02:03:50.310
There's Becca Levy's book "Breaking the Age Code."

2938
02:03:50.310 --> 02:03:52.020
Becca Levy is the one who actually...

2939
02:03:52.020 --> 02:03:55.490
In many ways our program is built on her work from Yale.

2940
02:03:55.490 --> 02:03:57.600
She was able to show that when you shift a person

2941
02:03:57.600 --> 02:04:00.050
from a negative to a neutral mindset.

2942
02:04:00.050 --> 02:04:03.840
I'm sorry, from a negative to a positive mindset on aging,

2943
02:04:03.840 --> 02:04:07.682
you give them 7.5 additional years of life.

2944
02:04:07.682 --> 02:04:09.470
What?

2945
02:04:09.470 --> 02:04:10.620
It's actually more life

2946
02:04:10.620 --> 02:04:13.160
than if someone stopped smoking at age 50,

2947
02:04:13.160 --> 02:04:17.480
or if they started exercising at age 50.

2948
02:04:17.480 --> 02:04:20.450
So the public health benefit of shifting a mindset

2949
02:04:20.450 --> 02:04:23.700
around aging has a greater benefit to society

2950
02:04:23.700 --> 02:04:25.900
and people are happier.

2951
02:04:25.900 --> 02:04:28.200
And yet we have PSAs left and right

2952
02:04:28.200 --> 02:04:29.800
about how we should stop smoking,

2953
02:04:29.800 --> 02:04:32.320
and how we should start exercising.

2954
02:04:32.320 --> 02:04:35.430
We have no PSAs around how to reframe aging.

2955
02:04:35.430 --> 02:04:39.250
So her book "Breaking the Age Code,"

2956
02:04:39.250 --> 02:04:43.010
which just came out in the last month is a great book.

2957
02:04:43.010 --> 02:04:44.710
You could do, I mean online.

